export const paper = { year: 2002, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"A complete cycle of a traffic light takes 60 seconds. During each cycle, the light is green for 25 seconds, yellow for 5 seconds and red for 30 seconds. At a randomly chosen time, the probability that the light will not be green is:","A":"1/3","B":"1/4","C":"5/12","D":"7/12","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Total cycle = 60 seconds.\nGreen duration = 25 seconds.\nNot green = Yellow + Red = 5 + 30 = 35 seconds.\nProbability = 35/60 = 7/12.","revisionPoints":"Probability = Favourable outcomes / Total outcomes.\nHere, favourable duration = time when light is yellow or red.\n35/60 simplifies to 7/12."},
{"no":2,"text":"The last opportunity to avoid the partition of India was lost with the rejection of:","A":"Cripps Mission","B":"Rajagopalachari Formula","C":"Cabinet Mission","D":"Wavell Plan","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Cabinet Mission","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Cabinet Mission Plan of 1946 attempted to keep India united through a federal arrangement.\nIts failure deepened the political differences between the Congress and Muslim League.\nThe subsequent developments ultimately led towards Partition in 1947.","revisionPoints":"Cabinet Mission → 1946.\nMembers included Pethick-Lawrence, Stafford Cripps and A.V. Alexander.\nProposed a Union of India with limited central subjects.\nFailure of the plan was an important step towards Partition."},
{"no":3,"text":"With reference to India, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"About one-third of the area of the country records more than 750 mm of annual rainfall.","B":"The dominant source of irrigation in the country is wells.","C":"Alluvial soil is the predominant type of soil in the northern plains of the country.","D":"The mountain areas account for about thirty percent of the surface area of the country.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Rainfall","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source marks (a) as correct.\nIndia's rainfall is highly variable spatially.\nA significant portion of the country receives relatively low or moderate rainfall.","revisionPoints":"Indian rainfall is strongly influenced by the Southwest Monsoon.\nRainfall distribution is uneven across regions.\nWestern Rajasthan receives very low rainfall.\nWestern Ghats and parts of Northeast India receive very high rainfall."},
{"no":4,"text":"A solid cube just gets completely immersed in water when a 0.2 kg mass is placed on it. If the mass is removed, the cube is 2 cm above the water level. What is the length of each side of the cube?","A":"12 cm","B":"10 cm","C":"8 cm","D":"6 cm","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Let side of cube = x cm.\nWhen the extra mass is removed, the cube rises 2 cm.\nThe displaced-water volume corresponding to the additional immersion balances the 0.2 kg mass.\nUsing Archimedes' principle and the given dimensions leads to x = 10 cm.","revisionPoints":"Archimedes' principle: Buoyant force equals the weight of displaced fluid.\nFloating bodies displace a quantity of fluid whose weight equals their own weight.\nAdding a load increases the volume submerged."},
{"no":5,"text":"The historian Barani refused to consider the state in India under the Delhi Sultans as truly Islamic because:","A":"The majority of the population did not follow Islam.","B":"Muslim theologians were often disregarded.","C":"The Sultan supplemented Muslim law by framing his own regulations.","D":"Religious freedom was accorded to non-Muslims.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Delhi Sultanate","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Ziauddin Barani was a major historian of the Delhi Sultanate.\nHe emphasised the role of Islamic religious law and theologians in governance.\nThe source identifies the disregard of Muslim theologians as the reason.","revisionPoints":"Ziauddin Barani → important historian of the Delhi Sultanate.\nMajor work → Tarikh-i-Firoz Shahi.\nAssociated with the reigns of Muhammad bin Tughlaq and Firuz Shah Tughlaq.\nMedieval historians often connected political authority with religious legitimacy."},
{"no":6,"text":"Who among the following won the men's singles title at the World Badminton Championship in 2001?","A":"Gopichand","B":"Hendrawan","C":"Ji Xin Peng","D":"Peter Gade","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Badminton","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Hendrawan of Indonesia won the men's singles title at the 2001 World Badminton Championships.\nHe defeated Peter Gade in the final.","revisionPoints":"2001 World Badminton Championship → Hendrawan.\nHendrawan → Indonesia.\nSports questions often test major international winners from a specific year."},
{"no":7,"text":"With reference to the latest developments in stem cell research, consider:\nThe only source of human stem cells are embryos at blastocyst stage.\nStem cells can be derived without causing destruction to blastocysts.\nStem cells can regenerate themselves in vitro virtually forever.\nIndian research centres have also created a few cell lines which can develop into many types of tissues.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Stem Cell Research","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Human stem cells are not obtained only from blastocyst-stage embryos.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect according to the source framing.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Stem cells have the capacity for extensive self-renewal.\nStatement 4 — Correct: Indian research centres had developed cell lines capable of differentiating into different tissue types.","revisionPoints":"Stem cells have two key properties:\nSelf-renewal\nDifferentiation\nEmbryonic stem cells are pluripotent.\nAdult/somatic stem cells also exist.\nStem-cell research has applications in regenerative medicine."},
{"no":8,"text":"In the year 2001, NASA launched a spacecraft to Mars named:","A":"Mars Climate Orbiter","B":"Mars Global Surveyor","C":"Mars Odyssey","D":"Mars Polar Lander","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Mars Missions","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"NASA launched 2001 Mars Odyssey in April 2001.\nIt entered orbit around Mars in 2001.\nIts mission included studying the Martian surface and environment.","revisionPoints":"Mars Odyssey → launched in 2001.\nNASA → United States space agency.\nMars missions commonly study:\nSurface composition\nAtmosphere\nWater/ice evidence\nPlanetary geology"},
{"no":9,"text":"Consider the following climatic and geographical phenomena:\nCondensation\nHigh temperature and humidity\nOrography\nVertical wind\nThundercloud development is due to which of these phenomena?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Thunderclouds","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Thunderclouds develop through strong convection.\nHigh temperature causes strong upward movement of warm air.\nHigh humidity provides abundant water vapour.\nCondensation occurs as rising air cools.\nThe source marks (a).","revisionPoints":"Thunderstorms are associated with cumulonimbus clouds.\nWarm, moist and unstable air favours thunderstorm development.\nCondensation releases latent heat, strengthening convection.\nThunderstorms may produce heavy rain, lightning and strong winds."},
{"no":10,"text":"A trader fixed the price of an article in such a way that by giving a 10% rebate on the fixed price he made a 15% profit. If the cost of the article is ₹72, the fixed price is:","A":"₹82.80","B":"₹90.00","C":"₹92.00","D":"₹97.80","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Cost Price = ₹72.\nProfit = 15%.\nSelling Price = 72 × 115/100 = ₹82.80.\nA 10% rebate means selling price = 90% of marked price.\nMarked Price = 82.80 ÷ 0.90 = ₹92.","revisionPoints":"Profit percentage is calculated on cost price.\nDiscount/rebate percentage is calculated on marked price.\nFormula:\nSP = CP × (100 + Profit%)/100\nSP = MP × (100 − Discount%)/100"},
{"no":11,"text":"Among living organisms, which one of the following is the most responsible factor for bringing about the origin of a new species?","A":"Isolation","B":"Mutation","C":"Natural selection","D":"Sexual reproduction","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Evolution","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Mutations introduce new genetic variations into populations.\nSuch variations provide the raw material upon which evolutionary processes can act.\nOver generations, genetic divergence can contribute to speciation.","revisionPoints":"Mutation → source of new genetic variation.\nNatural selection → favours advantageous heritable variations.\nIsolation → can prevent gene flow between populations.\nSpeciation generally involves variation + reproductive isolation + evolutionary divergence."},
{"no":12,"text":"For short-term climate prediction, which event detected in the last decade was associated with occasional weak monsoon rains in the Indian subcontinent?","A":"La Niña","B":"Movement of Jet Streams","C":"El Niño and Southern Oscillation","D":"Greenhouse effect at the global level","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["El Nino","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"ENSO is a major coupled ocean-atmosphere phenomenon in the tropical Pacific.\nEl Niño events can influence atmospheric circulation and Indian monsoon behaviour.\nThe source associates it with occasional weak monsoon rainfall.","revisionPoints":"El Niño → abnormal warming of central/eastern tropical Pacific.\nLa Niña → abnormal cooling of the same region.\nENSO influences global atmospheric circulation.\nIndian monsoon variability is influenced by several factors, including ENSO."},
{"no":13,"text":"Four Indian Ocean region islands are marked as 1, 2, 3 and 4:\nA. Seychelles\nB. Chagos\nC. Mauritius\nD. Socotra\nMatch them with the map and select the correct code.","A":"1 3 4 2","B":"3 1 2 4","C":"1 3 2 4","D":"3 1 4 2","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Indian Ocean Islands","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The question depends on the map, which is not reproduced in the text extraction.\nThe source explicitly gives the answer as (d).\nTherefore, the map-based matching is retained from the source.","revisionPoints":"Seychelles → western Indian Ocean.\nChagos Archipelago → central Indian Ocean.\nMauritius → southwest Indian Ocean.\nSocotra → near the Horn of Africa, close to the Arabian Sea."},
{"no":14,"text":"The World Development Report is an annual publication of:","A":"United Nations Development Programme","B":"International Bank for Reconstruction and Development","C":"World Trade Organisation","D":"International Monetary Fund","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["World Bank","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The World Development Report is published by the World Bank.\nThe International Bank for Reconstruction and Development (IBRD) is part of the World Bank Group.\nThe report focuses on major development-related economic and social issues.","revisionPoints":"World Development Report → World Bank.\nIBRD → International Bank for Reconstruction and Development.\nIMF → focuses mainly on international monetary and financial stability.\nWTO → deals primarily with international trade rules."},
{"no":15,"text":"Match the following ancient sites with their archaeological findings:\nAncient Site\nArchaeological Finding\nA. Lothal\n1. Ploughed field\nB. Kalibangan\n2. Dockyard\nC. Dholavira\n3. Terracotta replica of a plough\nD. —\n4. An inscription comprising ten large-sized signs of the Harappan script\nCodes:","A":"1 2 3 4","B":"2 1 4 3","C":"1 2 4 3","D":"2 1 3 4","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Indus Valley Civilization","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Lothal → Dockyard\nKalibangan → Ploughed field\nDholavira → inscription/signboard with large Harappan signs\nThe source's formatting has an incomplete entry for D, but the answer key is (b).","revisionPoints":"Lothal → famous Harappan dockyard.\nKalibangan → evidence of an ancient ploughed field.\nDholavira → major Harappan site in Gujarat.\nHarappan civilisation → important for urban planning, drainage and craft production."},
{"no":16,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The status of Gomateshwara at Shravanabelagola represents the last Tirthankara of Jains.","B":"India's largest Buddhist monastery is in Arunachal Pradesh.","C":"The Khajuraho temples were built under Chandela kings.","D":"The Hoysalesvara temple is dedicated to Siva.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient & Medieval India","tags":["Jain Statues","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source gives (a) as the answer.\nImportant source note: the wording is problematic. Gomateshwara/Bahubali is not a Tirthankara; he is associated with Jain tradition as the son of Rishabhanatha.\nTherefore, this question's supplied answer key appears inconsistent with the historical terminology.","revisionPoints":"Gomateshwara/Bahubali statue → Shravanabelagola, Karnataka.\nKhajuraho temples → associated with the Chandela dynasty.\nHoysalesvara Temple → Shiva, located at Halebidu.\nFor source-based preparation, distinguish the printed answer key from historically accurate facts."},
{"no":17,"text":"Open stunted forests with bushes and small trees having long roots and sharp thorns or spines are commonly found in:","A":"Eastern Orissa","B":"North-eastern Tamil Nadu","C":"Siwaliks and Terai region","D":"Western Andhra Pradesh","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Forest Types","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The vegetation described corresponds to tropical thorn and scrub vegetation.\nSuch vegetation occurs in regions with relatively low rainfall and dry conditions.\nThe source identifies western Andhra Pradesh.","revisionPoints":"Thorn forests → generally associated with arid and semi-arid regions.\nTypical adaptations:\nLong roots\nSmall leaves\nThorns/spines\nReduced transpiration\nImportant species include acacia and euphorbia."},
{"no":18,"text":"Match the following Articles of the Indian Constitution with their provisions:\nArticle\nProvision\nA. Article 16(2)\n1. No person shall be deprived of property save by authority of law\nB. Article 29(2)\n2. No discrimination in public appointment on grounds of race, religion or caste\nC. Article 30(1)\n3. Minorities have the right to establish and administer educational institutions\nD. Article 31(1)\n4. No citizen shall be denied admission into State educational institutions on specified grounds\nCodes:","A":"2 4 3 1","B":"3 1 2 4","C":"2 1 3 4","D":"3 4 2 1","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Equality","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Article 16(2) → 2: Equality of opportunity in public employment.\nArticle 29(2) → 4: No denial of admission on specified grounds.\nArticle 30(1) → 3: Minority educational institutions.\nArticle 31(1) → 1: Protection relating to deprivation of property under the constitutional provision as it existed then.","revisionPoints":"Article 14 → Equality before law.\nArticle 16 → Equality of opportunity in public employment.\nArticle 29 → Protection of interests of minorities/cultural groups.\nArticle 30 → Minority educational institutions.\nArticle 31 was later removed from the list of Fundamental Rights by the 44th Amendment."},
{"no":19,"text":"In ancient Indian Buddhist monasteries, a ceremony called Pavarana used to be held. It was the:","A":"Occasion to elect the Sanghaparinayaka and two speakers.","B":"Confession by monks of offences committed during their stay in monasteries during the rainy season.","C":"Ceremony of initiation of a new person into the Buddhist Sangha.","D":"Gathering of Buddhist monks on the day after the full moon of Ashadha when they took up residence for the rainy season.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Buddhism","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Pavarana was associated with the conclusion of the Buddhist rainy-season retreat.\nMonks were expected to invite one another to point out any faults or offences.\nIt was therefore associated with confession and mutual admonition.","revisionPoints":"Pavarana → Buddhist monastic observance.\nAssociated with the end of Vassa/rainy-season retreat.\nVassa → period when monks traditionally remain in residence during the rainy season.\nBuddhist monastic discipline is contained in the Vinaya Pitaka."},
{"no":20,"text":"Consider the volumes of the following:\nA parallelepiped of length 5 cm, breadth 3 cm and height 4 cm\nA cube of each side 4 cm\nA cylinder of radius 3 cm and length 3 cm\nA sphere of radius 3 cm\nThe volumes in decreasing order are:","A":"I, III, II, IV","B":"IV, II, III, I","C":"I, II, III, IV","D":"IV, III, II, I","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"I — Parallelepiped:\\(V = 5 × 3 × 4 = 60\\text{ cm}^3\\)\nII — Cube:\\(V = 4^3 = 64\\text{ cm}^3\\)\nIII — Cylinder:\\(V = πr^2h = π × 3^2 × 3 = 27π ≈ 84.8\\text{ cm}^3\\)\nIV — Sphere:\\(V = \\frac{4}{3}π(3)^3 = 36π ≈ 113.1\\text{ cm}^3\\)\nTherefore: IV > III > II > I.","revisionPoints":"Cuboid/parallelepiped → \\(lbh\\)\nCube → \\(a^3\\)\nCylinder → \\(πr^2h\\)\nSphere → \\(\\frac{4}{3}πr^3\\)\nAlways calculate/compare numerical values before selecting the decreasing order."},
{"no":21,"text":"The Draft Electricity Bill, 2001 seeks to replace:\nIndian Electricity Act, 1910\nElectricity (Supply) Act, 1948\nElectricity Regulatory Commission Act, 1998\nState Electricity Boards\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"1, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy Policy","tags":["Electricity Act","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Draft Electricity Bill, 2001 proposed to replace the three existing electricity laws:\nIndian Electricity Act, 1910\nElectricity (Supply) Act, 1948\nElectricity Regulatory Commission Act, 1998\nHence statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Electricity sector legislation was consolidated through later reforms.\nThe Electricity Act, 2003 became the major comprehensive legislation.\nImportant themes:\nGeneration\nTransmission\nDistribution\nRegulation\nCompetition"},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nWIPO is a specialised agency of the United Nations system.\nWIPO has its headquarters at Rome.\nTRIPS Agreement is binding on all WTO members.\nLeast developed country members of WTO are not required to apply TRIPS provisions for 20 years from the general date of application.\nWhich are correct?","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"1 and 3","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Organizations","tags":["WIPO","TRIPS","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: WIPO is a specialised agency of the UN.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: WIPO headquarters is in Geneva, Switzerland, not Rome.\nStatement 3 — Correct: TRIPS is part of the WTO framework.\nStatement 4 — Incorrect: The source does not accept the stated blanket 20-year exemption.","revisionPoints":"WIPO → World Intellectual Property Organization.\nHeadquarters → Geneva.\nTRIPS → Trade-Related Aspects of Intellectual Property Rights.\nWTO members are subject to the WTO agreements, including TRIPS."},
{"no":23,"text":"India had the maximum volume of foreign trade with:","A":"USA","B":"Japan","C":"Germany","D":"UAE","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["India's Trade Partners","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies the USA as India's largest foreign-trade partner among the given options for the period relevant to the question.\nThe question is specifically based on the trade situation of that period.","revisionPoints":"Foreign trade = exports + imports.\nTrade-partner rankings change over time.\nAlways consider the reference year in economy questions."},
{"no":24,"text":"Which one of the following is a lagoon?","A":"Ashtamudi Lake","B":"Chilka Lake","C":"Periyar Lake","D":"Pulicat Lake","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Lagoons","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source marks (c) as the answer.\nThe wording of this question/key is problematic from standard geographical classification, since Chilika and Pulicat are well-known coastal lagoons.\nTherefore, for this paper, retain the source answer while noting the classification issue.","revisionPoints":"Lagoon → shallow water body separated from the sea by a barrier, sand spit or similar coastal feature.\nChilika → famous brackish-water coastal lagoon in Odisha.\nPulicat → coastal lagoon between Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu.\nCoastal lagoon questions require careful distinction from inland lakes."},
{"no":25,"text":"Ticks and mites are actually:","A":"Arachnids","B":"Crustaceans","C":"Insects","D":"Myriapods","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Arachnids","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Ticks and mites belong to the class Arachnida.\nThey are therefore related to spiders and scorpions.\nThey are not insects because insects have three pairs of legs, while adult arachnids generally have four pairs.","revisionPoints":"Arachnida → spiders, scorpions, ticks and mites.\nArachnids generally have:\n8 legs\nTwo major body regions\nNo antennae\nInsects → generally 6 legs."},
{"no":26,"text":"With reference to blood in a normal person, which statement is correct?","A":"Veins are less numerous and hold less blood than arteries.","B":"Blood cells constitute about 70% of total blood volume.","C":"WBCs are made only by lymph nodes.","D":"Blood has more platelets than WBCs.","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Human Blood","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Platelets are present in the blood in much larger numbers than white blood cells.\n(a) is incorrect because veins contain a large proportion of the body's blood.\n(b) is incorrect because plasma constitutes roughly 55% of blood volume.\n(c) is incorrect because WBCs are produced in several tissues, especially bone marrow and lymphoid organs.","revisionPoints":"Blood consists mainly of:\nPlasma\nRBCs\nWBCs\nPlatelets\nPlatelets → important for blood clotting.\nWBCs → major role in immunity.\nRBCs → transport oxygen."},
{"no":27,"text":"Which statement is correct with reference to our solar system?","A":"Earth is the densest of all planets in the solar system.","B":"The predominant element in Earth is silicon.","C":"The Sun contains 75% of the mass of the solar system.","D":"The diameter of the Sun is 190 times that of Earth.","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["Solar System","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Earth's average density is approximately 5.51 g/cm³.\nIt is the densest planet in the Solar System.\n(b) is incorrect because Earth's composition includes large amounts of iron, oxygen, silicon and magnesium; silicon is not the predominant element by mass.\n(c) understates the Sun's share of the Solar System's mass.\n(d) is incorrect; the Sun's diameter is roughly 109 times Earth's diameter.","revisionPoints":"Earth → densest planet.\nJupiter → largest planet.\nMercury → smallest planet.\nSun contains the overwhelming majority of the Solar System's mass."},
{"no":28,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"The boiling point of an aqueous solution is higher than that of pure water.","B":"Addition of solutes causes an increase in water potential.","C":"Vapour pressure of water in a solution is lower than that of pure water.","D":"Water movement through a semipermeable membrane can never be prevented by applying pressure.","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Colligative Properties","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source marks (b).\nHowever, the wording is scientifically problematic: addition of solute generally lowers water potential, not increases it.\nThe scientifically standard statement among these is that dissolved solutes lower the vapour pressure of the solvent.","revisionPoints":"Solute addition generally:\nLowers vapour pressure.\nRaises boiling point.\nLowers freezing point.\nLowers water potential.\nOsmosis involves movement of water across a semipermeable membrane.\nOsmotic movement can be opposed by applying sufficient external pressure."},
{"no":29,"text":"With reference to Indian agriculture, which statement is correct?","A":"About 90% of the area under pulses in India is rainfed.","B":"The share of pulses in gross cropped area has doubled in the last two decades.","C":"India accounts for about 15% of the world's total area under rice.","D":"Rice occupies about 34% of India's gross cropped area.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Pulses","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Pulses are largely cultivated under rainfed conditions.\nIrrigation coverage for pulses has historically been lower than for major irrigated crops such as rice and wheat.\nThe source identifies (a) as correct.","revisionPoints":"Pulses include:\nGram\nTur/arhar\nUrad\nMoong\nLentil\nPulses are important sources of plant protein.\nLarge parts of pulse cultivation depend on monsoon rainfall."},
{"no":30,"text":"With reference to Sufism in Indian history, consider:\nShaikh Ahmad Sirhindi was a contemporary of Ibrahim Lodi.\nShaikh Nasiruddin Chiragh-i-Dehlavi was a disciple of Shaikh Nizamuddin Auliya.\nAurangzeb was a contemporary of Shaikh Salim Chishti.\nThe Qadiri order was first introduced in India by Shaikh Niamatullah and Makhdum Muhammad Jilani.\nWhich are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"2 and 4","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Sufism","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Shaikh Ahmad Sirhindi belonged to the Mughal period, not the time of Ibrahim Lodi.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Nasiruddin Chiragh-i-Dehlavi was associated with Nizamuddin Auliya's Chishti tradition.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: Salim Chishti belonged to the Akbar period, much earlier than Aurangzeb.\nStatement 4 — Correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Chishti order → major Sufi order in medieval India.\nNizamuddin Auliya → prominent Chishti saint.\nShaikh Ahmad Sirhindi → associated with Naqshbandi tradition.\nSalim Chishti → associated with Akbar's reign.\nQadiri order → another important Sufi order in India."},
{"no":31,"text":"The salaries and allowances of the Judges of a High Court are charged to:","A":"Consolidated Fund of India","B":"Consolidated Fund of the State","C":"Contingency Fund of India","D":"Contingency Fund of the State","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["High Court Judges","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"High Courts are institutions of the State judiciary.\nThe salaries and allowances of High Court judges are charged on the Consolidated Fund of the State.\nThis expenditure is therefore not subject to voting in the State Legislature.","revisionPoints":"High Court judges → expenditure charged on State Consolidated Fund.\nSupreme Court judges → expenditure charged on Consolidated Fund of India.\nCharged expenditure → not submitted to vote of the legislature."},
{"no":32,"text":"Consider the following plants:\nBougainvillea\nCarnations\nCocoa\nGrapes\nWhich are propagated by stem cuttings?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Botany","tags":["Plant Propagation","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies all four plants.\nStem cutting is a form of vegetative propagation.\nA new plant develops from a stem portion of the parent plant under suitable conditions.","revisionPoints":"Vegetative propagation → asexual reproduction in plants.\nCommon methods:\nStem cutting\nLayering\nGrafting\nBudding\nAdvantages include producing plants genetically similar to the parent."},
{"no":33,"text":"The members of the Constituent Assembly which drafted the Constitution of India were:","A":"Nominated by the British Parliament","B":"Nominated by the Governor-General","C":"Elected by the Legislative Assemblies of various provinces","D":"Elected by the Indian National Congress and Muslim League","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Constituent Assembly","tags":["Constituent Assembly","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Constituent Assembly was formed under the Cabinet Mission Plan, 1946.\nProvincial representatives were indirectly elected by the provincial legislative assemblies.\nRepresentatives of princely states were nominated by their rulers.","revisionPoints":"Constituent Assembly → formed under Cabinet Mission Plan.\nProvincial members → indirectly elected.\nPrincely State representatives → nominated.\nConstitution was adopted on 26 November 1949.\nConstitution came into force on 26 January 1950."},
{"no":34,"text":"A train of length 150 metres, moving at 90 km/hr, can cross a 200-metre bridge in:","A":"8 seconds","B":"14 seconds","C":"6 seconds","D":"15 seconds","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Total distance to be covered:\nTrain length + bridge length\n= 150 + 200 = 350 m\nSpeed = 90 km/hr.\nConvert to m/s:\n90 × 5/18 = 25 m/s\nTime = Distance / Speed:\n350 ÷ 25 = 14 seconds.","revisionPoints":"Train crossing a bridge → distance = train length + bridge length.\nConversion:\nkm/hr → m/s = × 5/18\nm/s → km/hr = × 18/5"},
{"no":35,"text":"The ancient Indian play Mudrarakshasa by Vishakhadatta deals with:","A":"Conflict between Gods and demons","B":"Romantic story of an Aryan prince and tribal woman","C":"Power struggle between two Aryan tribes","D":"Court intrigues at the time of Chandragupta Maurya","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Mudrarakshasa","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Mudrarakshasa is a Sanskrit political drama.\nIt deals with the political struggle surrounding the establishment of Chandragupta Maurya's rule.\nChanakya/Kautilya and Rakshasa are central figures in the political narrative.","revisionPoints":"Mudrarakshasa → author Vishakhadatta.\nHistorical setting → rise of Chandragupta Maurya.\nImportant characters → Chanakya and Rakshasa.\nIt is an important source for understanding the political atmosphere associated with the Mauryan rise."},
{"no":36,"text":"India's external debt increased from US$98,158 million at the end of March 2000 to US$100,225 million at the end of March 2001 due to an increase in:","A":"Multilateral and bilateral debt","B":"Rupee debt","C":"Commercial borrowings and NRI deposits","D":"Borrowing from IMF","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["External Debt","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source attributes the increase to multilateral and bilateral debt.\nExternal debt can be classified according to the source of borrowing.\nMultilateral debt involves institutions involving multiple countries; bilateral debt involves borrowing between two governments.","revisionPoints":"External debt → liabilities owed to foreign creditors.\nMajor categories include:\nMultilateral debt\nBilateral debt\nCommercial borrowings\nNRI deposits\nDebt statistics must be interpreted with reference to the specific year."},
{"no":37,"text":"Consider the following sectors:\nAgriculture, Forestry and Fishing\nManufacturing\nTrade, Hotels, Transport and Communication\nFinancing, Insurance, Real Estate and Business Services\nWhat is the decreasing order of their contribution to GDP at factor cost at constant prices during 2000–01?","A":"3, 1, 2, 4","B":"1, 3, 4, 2","C":"3, 4, 1, 2","D":"1, 3, 2, 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"National Income","tags":["GDP Sectoral Composition","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"According to the source, the sectors ranked:\nTrade, Hotels, Transport & Communication\nFinancing, Insurance, Real Estate & Business Services\nAgriculture, Forestry & Fishing\nManufacturing\nTherefore the answer is 3 → 4 → 1 → 2.","revisionPoints":"GDP can be analysed by sectoral contribution.\nMajor broad sectors:\nPrimary\nSecondary\nTertiary\nServices form a major component of India's economy."},
{"no":38,"text":"Who set a new national record in the women's pole vault event at the 7th edition of the Senior Federation Cup held in Karnataka in 2001?","A":"G.G. Pramila","B":"Jyothna Deka","C":"Karamjeet Kaur","D":"Manisha Dey","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Athletics","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Karamjeet Kaur.\nThe question is based on the sports achievements recorded in 2001.","revisionPoints":"Event → Women's pole vault.\nCompetition → Senior Federation Cup.\nYear → 2001.\nSports-current-affairs questions are strongly dependent on the year specified."},
{"no":39,"text":"The main reason for unrest and violence in Zimbabwe in recent years was:","A":"Conflict between different native ethnic groups","B":"Prolonged crisis over land reforms","C":"Economic crisis due to continuous drought and famine","D":"Power struggle between white European settlers and the native black community","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Zimbabwe","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Zimbabwe experienced major political and social tensions surrounding land redistribution.\nThe government's land reform programme involved the redistribution of predominantly white-owned commercial farms.\nThis created significant political, economic and social conflict.","revisionPoints":"Zimbabwe → southern Africa.\nLand question → major issue in post-colonial Zimbabwe.\nLand reform involved redistribution of large commercial farms.\nPolitical instability can be closely connected with land ownership and inequality."},
{"no":40,"text":"Match the following artists with their respective art forms:\nArtist\nArt\nA. Hiren Bhattacharya\n1. Bharatanatyam\nB. Malini Rajurkar\n2. Hindustani vocal music\nC. Pratibha Prahlad\n3. Kuchipudi\nD. Vempati Chinna Satyam\n4. Puppetry\nCodes:","A":"4 2 1 3","B":"3 1 2 4","C":"4 1 2 3","D":"3 2 1 4","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Performing Arts","tags":["Dance & Music","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Hiren Bhattacharya → Puppetry\nMalini Rajurkar → Hindustani vocal music\nPratibha Prahlad → Bharatanatyam\nVempati Chinna Satyam → Kuchipudi\nTherefore: 4–1–2–3.","revisionPoints":"Malini Rajurkar → Hindustani classical vocal.\nPratibha Prahlad → Bharatanatyam.\nVempati Chinna Satyam → major figure in Kuchipudi.\nArtist–art-form matching is a common UPSC culture question format."},
{"no":41,"text":"Consider the following natural phenomena:\nTerrestrial heating\nReflection of light\nRefraction of light\nDiffraction of light\nDue to which of these phenomena is a mirage formed?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1 and 3","D":"4 only","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Mirage","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Terrestrial heating heats the air close to the Earth's surface.\nThis creates layers of air having different temperatures and densities.\nRefraction of light occurs when light passes through these layers.\nThe bending of light creates the optical illusion known as a mirage.\nReflection and diffraction are not the primary causes of a mirage.","revisionPoints":"Mirage → mainly associated with refraction of light.\nUnequal heating of the ground produces temperature differences in air layers.\nDifferent air densities cause light to bend.\nCommonly observed on hot roads and deserts."},
{"no":42,"text":"HINDALCO, an aluminium factory located at Renukut, owes its site basically to:","A":"Proximity of raw materials","B":"Abundant supply of power","C":"Efficient transport network","D":"Proximity to the market","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Industrial Location","tags":["Aluminium Industry","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Aluminium production is a highly electricity-intensive industry.\nLarge quantities of electricity are required during the aluminium smelting process.\nTherefore, availability of abundant and relatively cheap power is an important factor in locating aluminium industries.\nThe source identifies abundant supply of power as the main reason for the location of HINDALCO at Renukut.","revisionPoints":"Aluminium industry → power-intensive industry.\nLocation factors:\nElectricity availability\nRaw materials\nTransport\nWater supply\nPower availability is particularly important for aluminium smelting."},
{"no":43,"text":"In the context of organic evolution, the loss of limbs of snakes is explained by the phenomenon of:","A":"Use and disuse of organs","B":"Adaptation to living in burrows","C":"Natural selection","D":"Inheritance of acquired characters","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Evolution","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The question uses the Lamarckian concept of evolution.\nAccording to Lamarck's theory:\nFrequently used organs become stronger.\nOrgans that are not used gradually degenerate.\nThe source explains the loss of limbs in snakes through the use and disuse of organs.\nThis reflects the historical Lamarckian interpretation of evolutionary change.","revisionPoints":"Lamarckism → use and disuse of organs.\nLamarck also proposed inheritance of acquired characters.\nDarwinism → evolution through natural selection.\nThe question reflects a historical evolutionary explanation."},
{"no":44,"text":"The purpose of the inclusion of Directive Principles of State Policy in the Indian Constitution is to establish:","A":"Political democracy","B":"Social democracy","C":"Gandhian democracy","D":"Social and economic democracy","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Directive Principles","tags":["Directive Principles of State Policy","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Fundamental Rights primarily provide the framework for political democracy.\nDirective Principles guide the State in establishing a just social and economic order.\nThey aim at reducing inequalities and promoting social welfare.\nTherefore, the source identifies their objective as social and economic democracy.","revisionPoints":"DPSPs → Part IV of the Constitution.\nDPSPs guide the State in policy-making.\nAim → social and economic justice.\nThey are fundamental in governance but are not enforceable by courts."},
{"no":45,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe objectives of the National Renewal Fund, set up in February 1992, were:\nTo give training and counselling for workers affected by retrenchment or VRS.\nRedeployment of workers.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Neither 1 nor 2","B":"Both 1 and 2","C":"1 only","D":"2 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industrial Policy","tags":["National Renewal Fund","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The National Renewal Fund was associated with measures to deal with the effects of industrial restructuring.\nTraining and counselling were intended to help workers affected by retrenchment or voluntary retirement.\nRedeployment of workers was also an objective.\nHence, both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"National Renewal Fund → established in 1992.\nFocus → workers affected by industrial restructuring.\nImportant measures:\nTraining\nCounselling\nRedeployment\nVRS → Voluntary Retirement Scheme."},
{"no":46,"text":"Which one of the following committees recommended the abolition of reservation of items for the small-scale sector in industry?","A":"Abid Hussain Committee","B":"Narasimham Committee","C":"Nayak Committee","D":"Rakesh Mohan Committee","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industrial Policy","tags":["Abid Hussain Committee","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Abid Hussain Committee examined issues related to the small-scale industrial sector.\nIt recommended removing the reservation of certain products exclusively for small-scale industries.\nThe recommendation was associated with greater competition and liberalisation of industry.","revisionPoints":"Abid Hussain Committee → small-scale industries.\nIndustrial reforms → emphasis on competition and liberalisation.\nProduct reservation was historically used to protect small-scale industries.\nCommittee-based questions often require linking the committee with its specific recommendation."},
{"no":47,"text":"India's population growth during the 20th century can be classified into four distinct phases. Match List I with List II.\nList I – Period\nList II – Phase\nA. 1901–1921\n1. Steady growth\nB. 1921–1951\n2. Rapid high growth\nC. 1951–1981\n3. Stagnant growth\nD. 1981–2001\n4. High growth with definite signs of slowdown\nCodes:","A":"3 1 4 2","B":"1 3 2 4","C":"3 1 2 4","D":"1 3 4 2","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Demography","tags":["Population Growth of India","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"1901–1921 → Stagnant growth\n1921–1951 → Steady growth\n1951–1981 → Rapid high growth\n1981–2001 → High growth with definite signs of slowdown\nTherefore, the correct sequence is 3–1–2–4.","revisionPoints":"1901–1921 → stagnant growth.\n1921 is considered an important demographic turning point.\n1951–1981 → period of rapid population growth.\n1981–2001 → high growth but with signs of slowing down.\nNote: The source text contains an OCR error referring to the “29th century”; the periods clearly indicate the 20th century."},
{"no":48,"text":"Who was the first Indian actress to receive the Padma Shri Award?","A":"Smita Patil","B":"Nargis Dutt","C":"Meena Kumari","D":"Madhubala","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["Padma Shri","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Nargis Dutt as the first Indian actress to receive the Padma Shri.\nThe award is one of India's major civilian honours.\nSuch questions require remembering important personalities associated with national awards.","revisionPoints":"Padma awards → major civilian honours of India.\nPadma Shri is one of the four Padma award categories.\nNargis Dutt → noted Indian film actress.\nAward/personality matching is a common static GK area."},
{"no":49,"text":"With reference to medieval Indian rulers, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Alauddin Khalji first set up a separate Ariz's department.","B":"Balban introduced the branding system of horses of the military.","C":"Muhammad bin Tughlaq was succeeded by his uncle to the Delhi throne.","D":"Firuz Tughlaq set up a separate department of slaves.","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Delhi Sultanate","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Firuz Tughlaq established a separate department concerned with slaves.\nThe source therefore identifies option (d) as correct.\nThe other options associate administrative or military developments with the wrong rulers.","revisionPoints":"Firuz Tughlaq → known for several administrative and welfare measures.\nHe maintained a very large body of slaves.\nMedieval administration → important themes include:\nMilitary organisation\nRevenue administration\nDepartments\nMarket regulations\nMatch rulers with specific administrative innovations."},
{"no":50,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nThe objectives of the National Renewal Fund, set up in February 1992, were:\nTo give training and counselling for workers affected by retrenchment or VRS.\nRedeployment of workers.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"Neither 1 nor 2","B":"Both 1 and 2","C":"1 only","D":"2 only","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Labour & Employment","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Training and counselling were included to assist workers affected by retrenchment or VRS.\nRedeployment was another objective.\nTherefore, both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"National Renewal Fund → 1992.\nTarget group → workers affected by industrial restructuring.\nKey objectives:\nTraining\nCounselling\nRedeployment\nVRS → Voluntary Retirement Scheme."},
{"no":51,"text":"The motive behind Shahjahan's Balkh campaign was to:","A":"Secure a friendly ruler in Balkh and Badakhshan which bordered Kabul","B":"Conquer Samarqand and Farghana, the Mughal homelands","C":"Fix the Mughal frontier on the 'scientific line', the Amu Daria","D":"Expand the Mughal Empire beyond the subcontinent","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Shahjahan","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Shah Jahan's Central Asian policy was connected with the Mughal dynasty's ancestral homeland.\nThe Balkh campaign was linked with the objective of recovering areas associated with Samarqand and Farghana.\nTherefore, the source identifies option (b).","revisionPoints":"Shah Jahan → Mughal emperor.\nBalkh campaign → Central Asian policy.\nSamarqand and Farghana → important regions associated with the Mughal ancestral background.\nMughal foreign policy extended beyond the Indian subcontinent."},
{"no":52,"text":"Consider the following high-yielding varieties of crops in India:\nArjun\nJaya\nPadma\nSonalika\nWhich of these are wheat?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 4","D":"3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["High Yielding Varieties","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Arjun is identified as a wheat variety.\nSonalika is also a wheat variety.\nTherefore, the correct combination is 1 and 4.\nJaya and Padma are not the wheat varieties identified by the source.","revisionPoints":"Arjun → wheat variety.\nSonalika → wheat variety.\nHYV → High Yielding Variety.\nHYV seeds were an important component of the Green Revolution."},
{"no":53,"text":"Which one of the following submitted in 1875 a petition to the House of Commons demanding India's direct representation in the British Parliament?","A":"The Deccan Association","B":"The Indian Association","C":"The Madras Mahajan Sabha","D":"The Poona Sarvajanik Sabha","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Early Nationalist Organisations","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies the Indian Association as the organisation that submitted the petition.\nThe demand reflected the growing political consciousness among educated Indians.\nSuch political associations preceded the formation of the Indian National Congress.","revisionPoints":"Indian Association → important early political organisation.\nPeriod → late 19th century.\nEarly political associations raised demands relating to:\nRepresentation\nCivil services\nConstitutional reforms\nIndian National Congress was established later, in 1885."},
{"no":54,"text":"The real intention of the British to include the princely states in the Federal Union proposed by the Government of India Act, 1935 was to:","A":"Exercise more and direct political and administrative control over the princely states","B":"Involve the princes actively in the administration of the colony","C":"Finally effect the complete political and administrative takeover of all the princely states by the British","D":"Use the princes to counterbalance the anti-imperialist doctrines of the nationalist leaders","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Government of India Act 1935","tags":["Princely States","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The proposed federation included representatives of the princely states.\nAccording to the source, the British intended to use the princes as a political counterweight to nationalist forces.\nThis would strengthen the position of the colonial government against the anti-imperialist movement.","revisionPoints":"Government of India Act, 1935 → proposed an All-India Federation.\nFederation involved:\nBritish Indian provinces\nPrincely states\nBritish constitutional strategy often sought to balance different political groups.\nPrincely states played an important role in the constitutional developments of the period."},
{"no":55,"text":"When one gene controls two or more different characters simultaneously, the phenomenon is called:","A":"Apomixis","B":"Pleiotropy","C":"Polyploidy","D":"Polytony","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Genetics","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Pleiotropy occurs when a single gene influences two or more different traits.\nThus, one gene can have multiple phenotypic effects.\nThe other options describe different genetic phenomena.","revisionPoints":"Pleiotropy → one gene → multiple characters.\nPolyploidy → presence of more than two complete sets of chromosomes.\nApomixis → asexual seed formation without normal fertilisation.\nGenetics questions often test precise definitions."},
{"no":56,"text":"Sun's halo is produced by the refraction of light in:","A":"Water vapour in Stratus clouds","B":"Ice crystals in Cirrocumulus clouds","C":"Ice crystals in Cirrus clouds","D":"Dust particles in Stratus clouds","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Sun's Halo","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Cirrus clouds occur at high altitudes.\nThey contain ice crystals.\nSunlight passing through these ice crystals undergoes optical phenomena such as refraction.\nThis produces the characteristic halo around the Sun.","revisionPoints":"Sun halo → associated with ice crystals.\nCirrus clouds → high-altitude clouds.\nIce crystals can produce halos and other optical effects.\nCloud classification is based partly on altitude and appearance."},
{"no":57,"text":"Which one of the following Articles of the Directive Principles of State Policy deals with the promotion of international peace and security?","A":"Article 51","B":"Article 48A","C":"Article 43A","D":"Article 41","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Directive Principles","tags":["Article 51","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Article 51 deals with the promotion of international peace and security.\nIt encourages the State to:\nPromote international peace and security.\nMaintain just and honourable relations between nations.\nFoster respect for international law.\nEncourage settlement of international disputes through arbitration.","revisionPoints":"Article 51 → International peace and security.\nArticle 48A → protection of environment and forests.\nArticle 43A → participation of workers in management.\nArticle 41 → right to work, education and public assistance within the State's capacity."},
{"no":58,"text":"Which one of the following rights was described by Dr. B. R. Ambedkar as the “heart and soul” of the Constitution?","A":"Right to freedom of religion","B":"Right to property","C":"Right to equality","D":"Right to Constitutional remedies","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Constitutional Remedies","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Dr. B. R. Ambedkar described the Right to Constitutional Remedies as the heart and soul of the Constitution.\nIt enables citizens to approach the courts when their Fundamental Rights are violated.\nArticle 32 provides this constitutional remedy before the Supreme Court.","revisionPoints":"Article 32 → Right to Constitutional Remedies.\nDr. B. R. Ambedkar → described Article 32 as the heart and soul of the Constitution.\nSupreme Court can issue constitutional writs.\nFundamental Rights become meaningful through enforceable remedies."},
{"no":59,"text":"With reference to the Wholesale Price Index (WPI), consider the following statements:\nThe new WPI series with base 1993–94 = 100 became effective from April 1998.\nIn the new WPI series, [the source text is OCR-garbled; it states that statements 2 and 3 are correct].\nThe weight for electricity has increased in the new WPI series.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1 and 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Inflation","tags":["Wholesale Price Index","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The supplied source identifies statements 2 and 3 as correct.\nStatement 1 is therefore not accepted according to the source's answer key.\nThe wording of statement 2 is incomplete/garbled in the supplied OCR text, so its exact original wording cannot be reliably reconstructed from this source alone.","revisionPoints":"WPI → important indicator of wholesale price movements.\nWPI uses a base year for comparison.\nElectricity is one of the items represented in the index.\nFor this question, follow the source's answer key, because statement 2 is corrupted in the supplied text."},
{"no":60,"text":"Match List I with List II:\nList I – Acts\nList II – Provisions\nA. Charter Act, 1813\n1. Set up a Board of Control in Britain to regulate East India Company's affairs\nB. Regulating Act\n2. Company's trade monopoly in India was ended\nC. Act of 1858\n3. Power to govern transferred from East India Company to British Crown\nD. Pitt's India Act\n4. Company's directors were asked to present correspondence and documents to the British Government\nCodes:","A":"2 4 3 1","B":"1 3 4 2","C":"2 3 4 1","D":"1 4 3 2","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["East India Company Acts","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Charter Act, 1813 → 2\nEnded the East India Company's trade monopoly in India, except certain specific areas.\nRegulating Act → 4\nRequired the Company to provide relevant correspondence/documents concerning administration.\nAct of 1858 → 3\nTransferred governance of India from the East India Company to the British Crown.\nPitt's India Act → 1\nEstablished the Board of Control to supervise Company affairs.","revisionPoints":"Regulating Act, 1773 → first major parliamentary control over the Company.\nPitt's India Act, 1784 → established Board of Control.\nCharter Act, 1813 → ended Company trade monopoly in India.\nGovernment of India Act, 1858 → Crown took over administration of India.\nThese Acts are important for understanding the gradual expansion of British parliamentary control over India."},
{"no":61,"text":"Match List I (Mangrove) with List II (State):\nMangrove\nState\nA. Achra Ratnagiri\n1. Karnataka\nB. Coondapur\n2. Kerala\nC. Pichavaram\n3. Andhra Pradesh\nD. Vembanad\n4. Maharashtra\n5. Tamil Nadu\nCodes:","A":"2 1 5 4","B":"4 5 3 2","C":"2 5 3 4","D":"4 1 5 2","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Mangroves","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Achra Ratnagiri → Maharashtra (4)\nCoondapur → Karnataka (1)\nPichavaram → Tamil Nadu (5)\nVembanad → Kerala (2)\nTherefore, the correct sequence is 4–1–5–2.","revisionPoints":"Pichavaram → important mangrove ecosystem in Tamil Nadu.\nVembanad → important wetland/backwater system in Kerala.\nMangroves are generally found in tidal coastal environments.\nThey help protect coastlines and provide important habitats."},
{"no":62,"text":"Which one of the following Acts of British India strengthened the Viceroy's authority over his Executive Council by substituting the “portfolio” or departmental system for corporate functioning?","A":"Indian Councils Act, 1861","B":"Government of India Act, 1858","C":"Indian Councils Act, 1892","D":"Indian Councils Act, 1909","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Indian Councils Act 1861","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Indian Councils Act, 1861 introduced the portfolio system.\nUnder this arrangement, different members of the Executive Council were assigned specific departments.\nThis replaced the earlier system of collective/corporate functioning.\nIt strengthened the administrative role of the Viceroy.","revisionPoints":"Indian Councils Act → important constitutional development during British rule.\n1861 → portfolio system.\nPortfolio system → allocation of specific departments to council members.\nImportant Acts to remember:\n1858 → Crown took over administration.\n1861 → portfolio system and legislative council changes.\n1892 → further expansion of legislative councils.\n1909 → Morley-Minto reforms."},
{"no":63,"text":"The age of a man is three times the sum of the ages of his two sons. Five years hence, his age will be double the sum of the ages of his sons. The father's present age is:","A":"40 years","B":"45 years","C":"50 years","D":"55 years","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Let the present sum of the sons' ages be x.\nFather's present age = 3x.\nAfter 5 years:\nFather's age = 3x + 5\nCombined age of two sons = x + 10\nAccording to the question:\n3x + 5 = 2(x + 10)\nTherefore:\n3x + 5 = 2x + 20\nx = 15\nFather's age = 3 × 15 = 45 years.","revisionPoints":"For age problems, define the combined age when individual ages are unnecessary.\nWhen there are two sons, their combined age increases by 10 years after 5 years.\nFather's age increases by only 5 years.\nAlways translate future-age statements into equations."},
{"no":64,"text":"The length of the longest pole that can be placed in a room 12 m long, 9 m wide and 8 m high is:","A":"12 m","B":"14 m","C":"17 m","D":"21 m","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The longest pole that can fit inside a rectangular room is its space diagonal.\nFormula:\n\\[ d=\\sqrt{l^2+b^2+h^2} \\]\nTherefore:\n\\[ d=\\sqrt{12^2+9^2+8^2} \\]\\[ =\\sqrt{144+81+64} \\]\\[ =\\sqrt{289}=17\\,m \\]","revisionPoints":"Cuboid space diagonal:\n√(l² + b² + h²)\nLongest object inside a rectangular room → space diagonal.\nDo not use the floor diagonal alone when height is also relevant."},
{"no":65,"text":"With reference to the human body, consider the following statements:\nThe production of somatotropin goes up when a person exercises.\nMen's testes produce progesterone.\nWomen's adrenal glands secrete testosterone.\nStress causes the adrenals to release very less amount of cortisol than usual.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"2, 4, 3, 1","B":"1, 3, 4, 2","C":"2, 3, 4, 1","D":"1, 4, 3, 2","ans":null,"category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Human Hormones","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source's answer key gives (d).\nHowever, the supplied OCR has the options corrupted: the option text appears to contain sequences such as “1, 4, 3, 2” rather than a normal statement-combination format.\nTherefore, the exact intended option structure cannot be reliably reconstructed from the supplied text.\nThe source should be treated as having an OCR/formatting issue for this question.","revisionPoints":"Somatotropin → growth hormone (GH).\nCortisol → important hormone involved in the body's response to stress.\nAdrenal glands → endocrine glands located above the kidneys.\nThe supplied question contains a formatting/OCR problem; verify the original paper before memorising the option structure.","dropped":true},
{"no":66,"text":"In the year 2001, the Prime Minister announced a five-year excise duty holiday for industries in:","A":"Cyclone-prone coastal Andhra Pradesh","B":"Border states of North-East","C":"Earthquake-ravaged Kutch district","D":"Recently formed states of Chhattisgarh and Jharkhand","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industrial Policy","tags":["Excise Duty Holiday","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Kutch district as the correct answer.\nThe measure was associated with encouraging industrial activity after the devastating earthquake.\nTax/excise incentives can be used as instruments for regional economic reconstruction.","revisionPoints":"Kutch → Gujarat.\n2001 → major earthquake affected the Kutch region.\nFiscal incentives can encourage:\nInvestment\nIndustrial reconstruction\nEmployment generation\nDisaster-affected regions may receive special economic packages."},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements with reference to India:\nThe Chief Election Commissioner and other Election Commissioners enjoy equal powers but receive unequal salaries.\nThe Chief Election Commissioner is entitled to the same salary as provided to a judge of the Supreme Court.\nThe Chief Election Commissioner shall not be removed from his office except in like manner and on like grounds as a judge of the Supreme Court.\nThe term of office of the Election Commissioner is five years from the date he assumes office or till the day he attains the age of 62 years, whichever is earlier.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 4","D":"2 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Election Commission","tags":["Chief Election Commissioner","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 2 — Correct according to the source: The CEC is entitled to a salary equivalent to that of a Supreme Court judge.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The CEC has special protection regarding removal and is removed in a manner similar to a Supreme Court judge.\nStatement 1 — Incorrect: The source does not accept the claim that the commissioners have equal powers but unequal salaries.\nStatement 4 — Incorrect: The stated five-year/62-year rule is not the correct rule in the source's framework.","revisionPoints":"Election Commission → Article 324.\nCEC has special protection against removal.\nCEC removal → procedure similar to removal of a Supreme Court judge.\nElection Commissioners are appointed by the President."},
{"no":68,"text":"In a company, 60% of the employees are men. Of these, 40% are drawing more than ₹50,000 per year. If 36% of the total employees draw more than ₹50,000 per year, what percentage among women are drawing less than ₹50,000 per year?","A":"70%","B":"60%","C":"40%","D":"30%","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assume total employees = 100.\nMen = 60.\nMen earning > ₹50,000 = 40% of 60 = 24.\nTotal employees earning > ₹50,000 = 36.\nTherefore, women earning > ₹50,000:\n36 − 24 = 12\nWomen = 40.\nWomen earning less than ₹50,000 = 40 − 12 = 28.\nPercentage:\n28/40 × 100 = 70%","revisionPoints":"Assume total population as 100 for percentage problems.\nMen = 60%; women = 40%.\n40% of men = 24% of total employees.\nWomen earning below ₹50,000 = 70% of women."},
{"no":69,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nLight of longer wavelength is scattered much more than light of shorter wavelength.\nThe speed of visible light in water is 0.95 times its speed in vacuum.\nRadio waves are produced by rapidly oscillating electrical currents.\nTo detect overspeeding vehicles, police use the Doppler effect of reflected short radio waves.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 4","D":"3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Doppler Effect","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Shorter wavelengths, such as blue/violet light, are scattered more strongly than longer wavelengths.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect according to the source: The stated value of 0.95 is not accepted.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Radio waves can be generated by oscillating electrical currents.\nStatement 4 — Correct: Police radar uses the Doppler effect to determine vehicle speed.","revisionPoints":"Rayleigh scattering → shorter wavelengths scatter more strongly.\nRadio waves → electromagnetic waves.\nDoppler effect → apparent change in frequency due to relative motion.\nRadar speed detection → Doppler principle."},
{"no":70,"text":"Global capital flows to developing countries increased significantly during the 1990s. In view of the East Asian financial crisis and Latin American experience, which type of inflow is good for the host country?","A":"Commercial loans","B":"Foreign Direct Investment","C":"Foreign Portfolio Investment","D":"External Commercial Borrowings","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Foreign Direct Investment","tags":["FDI vs FPI","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"FDI generally involves longer-term investment in productive assets and businesses.\nPortfolio flows can be more volatile and can leave a country rapidly.\nCommercial borrowing creates repayment obligations.\nThe source therefore identifies Foreign Direct Investment as the preferable inflow.","revisionPoints":"FDI → investment involving a lasting interest/control in an enterprise.\nFPI → investment in financial assets such as shares and securities.\nFPI is generally more volatile than FDI.\nCapital inflows can affect exchange rates, investment and financial stability."},
{"no":71,"text":"In terms of value, which one of the following commodities accounted for the largest agricultural exports by India during the three-year period from 1997–98 to 1999–2000?","A":"Cereals","B":"Marine products","C":"Spices","D":"Tea","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Agricultural Exports","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies marine products as the largest agricultural export by value during the specified period.\nAgricultural exports include both crop-based products and products from the fisheries sector.\nExport value is different from export quantity.","revisionPoints":"Marine products → important Indian export category.\nExport questions may ask about:\nQuantity\nValue\nGrowth rate\nDestination\nAlways pay attention to whether the question asks value or volume."},
{"no":72,"text":"In the year 2001, in which Women's Singles event did Venus Williams defeat Serena Williams to win the title?","A":"Australian Open 2001","B":"French Open 2001","C":"Wimbledon 2001","D":"US Open 2001","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Tennis","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies the US Open 2001 as the correct event.\nVenus Williams defeated Serena Williams in the final.\nThis was one of the notable Williams sisters' encounters in Grand Slam tennis.","revisionPoints":"US Open → one of the four Grand Slam tennis tournaments.\nFour Grand Slams:\nAustralian Open\nFrench Open\nWimbledon\nUS Open\nVenus and Serena Williams are sisters and major figures in women's tennis."},
{"no":73,"text":"The Consultative Committee of Members of Parliament for Railway Zones is constituted by the:","A":"President of India","B":"Ministry of Railways","C":"Ministry of Parliamentary Affairs","D":"Ministry of Transport","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliamentary Committees","tags":["Railway Consultative Committee","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies the Ministry of Parliamentary Affairs as the authority responsible for constituting the committee.\nSuch committees provide Members of Parliament an institutional mechanism for consultation on government activities.","revisionPoints":"Ministry of Parliamentary Affairs → important link between Parliament and government.\nParliamentary committees help facilitate:\nConsultation\nOversight\nExamination of government functioning\nRailway-related parliamentary consultation may involve members from different Houses."},
{"no":74,"text":"Which one of the following is monogamous?","A":"Wolf","B":"Walrus","C":"Seal","D":"Deer","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Animal Behaviour","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies the wolf as the monogamous animal among the options.\nMonogamy refers to a mating relationship involving one partner at a time, particularly in the context of reproduction.\nWolves are known for pair-bonding within packs.","revisionPoints":"Wolf → commonly associated with monogamous pair relationships.\nWalruses → generally polygynous social structure.\nAnimal behaviour questions may cover:\nMonogamy\nPolygamy\nTerritoriality\nParental care"},
{"no":75,"text":"The Five Year Plan in India is finally approved by:","A":"Union Cabinet","B":"President on the advice of Prime Minister","C":"Planning Commission","D":"National Development Council","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Planning","tags":["Five Year Plans","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"According to the institutional framework applicable when the question was asked, the National Development Council (NDC) gave final approval to Five Year Plans.\nThe Planning Commission was responsible for preparing the plans.\nThe NDC provided a wider forum involving the Union and States.","revisionPoints":"Planning Commission → prepared Five Year Plans.\nNational Development Council → approved Five Year Plans.\nNDC included participation from:\nPrime Minister\nUnion Ministers\nChief Ministers\nHistorical context: The Planning Commission and NDC framework belongs to the pre-NITI Aayog planning system."},
{"no":76,"text":"Consider the following countries of South Asia:\nBangladesh\nIndia\nPakistan\nSri Lanka\nThe descending order of literacy status of these countries is:","A":"4, 2, 1, 3","B":"2, 4, 3, 1","C":"4, 2, 3, 1","D":"2, 4, 1, 3","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Human Development","tags":["South Asia Literacy","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"According to the source's data for the period in question:\nSri Lanka → 4\nIndia → 2\nPakistan → 3\nBangladesh → 1\nTherefore, the descending order is Sri Lanka → India → Pakistan → Bangladesh.","revisionPoints":"Literacy comparisons depend on the year and dataset used.\nSri Lanka has historically recorded a relatively high literacy rate within South Asia.\nAlways check whether a question refers to:\nLiteracy rate\nAdult literacy\nFemale literacy\nOverall literacy"},
{"no":77,"text":"Among the following major stock exchanges of India, the exchange which recorded the highest turnover during 2000–01 was:","A":"Bombay Stock Exchange","B":"Calcutta Stock Exchange","C":"Delhi Stock Exchange","D":"National Stock Exchange","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Capital Markets","tags":["Stock Exchanges","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies the Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE) as having the highest turnover during 2000–01 among the listed options.\nStock-exchange turnover refers to the value/volume of transactions carried out through the exchange.","revisionPoints":"BSE → one of India's major stock exchanges.\nNSE → established in the 1990s and became a major organised securities market.\nTurnover → indicator of trading activity.\nThis question is historical, so rankings may differ from the present situation."},
{"no":78,"text":"A bus is moving at 30 km/hr ahead of a car moving at 50 km/hr. How many kilometres apart are they if it takes 15 minutes for the car to catch up with the bus?","A":"5 km","B":"7.5 km","C":"12.5 km","D":"15 km","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Relative speed of car with respect to bus:\n50 − 30 = 20 km/hr\nTime = 15 minutes = 15/60 = 1/4 hour.\nDistance between them:\n20 × 1/4 = 5 km","revisionPoints":"When two objects move in the same direction:\nRelative speed = difference of speeds.\nConvert minutes into hours before using km/hr.\nFormula:\nDistance = Relative speed × Time"},
{"no":79,"text":"The President of the Indian National Congress at the time of the partition of India was:","A":"C. Rajagopalachari","B":"J. B. Kripalani","C":"Jawaharlal Nehru","D":"Maulana Abul Kalam Azad","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Indian National Congress","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies J. B. Kripalani as the Congress President at the time of India's partition in 1947.\nHe served as Congress President during a crucial phase of the freedom movement and transition to independence.","revisionPoints":"J. B. Kripalani → Congress President around the time of independence.\n1947 → Indian independence and partition.\nMaulana Abul Kalam Azad → long-serving Congress leader and earlier Congress President.\nCongress sessions and presidents are important areas of modern Indian history."},
{"no":80,"text":"Which one of the following authorities recommends the principles governing the grants-in-aid of the revenues to the States out of the Consolidated Fund of India?","A":"Finance Commission","B":"Inter-State Council","C":"Union Ministry of Finance","D":"Public Accounts Committee","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Finance Commission","tags":["Grants-in-Aid","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Finance Commission is a constitutional body.\nIt recommends the principles governing the distribution of financial resources between the Union and the States.\nThis includes principles relating to grants-in-aid from the Consolidated Fund of India.\nTherefore, the correct answer is Finance Commission.","revisionPoints":"Finance Commission → Article 280.\nIt is constituted by the President.\nMajor functions include recommendations regarding:\nDistribution of taxes between Union and States\nGrants-in-aid\nMeasures to augment State funds for Panchayats and Municipalities\nFinance Commission is central to fiscal federalism."},
{"no":81,"text":"With reference to the colonial period of Indian history, match List I with List II:\nPerson\nEvent\nA. MacDonald\n1. Doctrine of Lapse\nB. Linlithgow\n2. Communal Award\nC. Dalhousie\n3. August Offer\nD. Chelmsford\n4. Dyarchy\nCodes:","A":"3 2 1 4","B":"3 2 4 1","C":"2 3 1 4","D":"2 3 4 1","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["Doctrine of Lapse","Communal Award","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"MacDonald → Communal Award (2).\nLinlithgow → August Offer (3).\nDalhousie → Doctrine of Lapse (1).\nChelmsford → Dyarchy (4).\nHence, the source gives the sequence 3–2–1–4.","revisionPoints":"Doctrine of Lapse → associated with Lord Dalhousie.\nCommunal Award → associated with Ramsay MacDonald.\nAugust Offer → made during Lord Linlithgow's tenure.\nDyarchy → introduced at the provincial level by the Government of India Act, 1919.\nSource note: The supplied answer key says (b), but the matching shown in the question itself gives MacDonald–2, Linlithgow–3, Dalhousie–1, Chelmsford–4, which corresponds to (c). This appears to be a source/key inconsistency."},
{"no":82,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nOcean currents are the slow surface movement of water in the ocean.\nOcean currents assist in maintaining the Earth's heat balance.\nOcean currents are set in motion primarily by prevailing winds.\nOcean currents are affected by the configuration of the ocean.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Oceanography","tags":["Ocean Currents","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Ocean currents involve the movement of seawater.\nThey redistribute heat between different regions of the Earth.\nPrevailing winds are an important factor driving surface currents.\nThe shape and configuration of ocean basins also influence the direction of currents.\nTherefore, all four statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Ocean currents → major mechanism of heat redistribution.\nSurface currents → strongly influenced by winds.\nOcean basin configuration → affects current direction.\nOcean currents influence climate, rainfall and marine ecosystems."},
{"no":83,"text":"Which country among the following has been involved in two [ties/related event in] Test cricket?","A":"Australia","B":"India","C":"West Indies","D":"England","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Cricket","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The wording of this question is garbled in the supplied source: “has been involved in two in Test cricket.”\nThe source's answer key identifies Australia.\nBecause the exact original wording is incomplete, the underlying fact cannot be reliably reconstructed from the supplied file alone.","revisionPoints":"Treat sports questions from old papers carefully because historical records are time-specific.\nThe supplied source supports Australia as the answer.\nExact wording should be verified against the original paper if this question is being used for memorisation."},
{"no":84,"text":"Match the organisations with their headquarters:\nOrganisation\nHeadquarters\nA. International Atomic Energy Agency\n1. Brussels\nB. International Telecommunication Union\n2. Geneva\nC. Council of the European Union\n3. Paris\nD. Organisation for Economic Cooperation and Development\n4. Vienna\nCodes:","A":"1 2 4 3","B":"1 3 4 2","C":"1 3 4 2","D":"4 2 1 3","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Organizations","tags":["IAEA","OECD","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"IAEA → Vienna (4).\nITU → Geneva (2).\nCouncil of the European Union → Brussels (1).\nOECD → Paris (3).\nHence, 4–2–1–3.","revisionPoints":"IAEA → Vienna.\nITU → Geneva.\nCouncil of the European Union → Brussels.\nOECD → Paris.\nHeadquarters-based questions are common in international organisations."},
{"no":85,"text":"With reference to Public Sector Undertakings in India, consider the following statements:\nMinerals and Metals Trading Corporation of India Limited is the largest non-oil importer of the country.\nProject and Equipment Corporation of India Limited is under the Ministry of Industry.\nOne objective of Export Credit Guarantee Corporation of India Limited is to enforce quality control and compulsory pre-shipment inspection of various exportable commodities.\nWhich of these is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Sector Undertakings","tags":["MMTC","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Statement 1 as correct.\nThe source does not accept Statements 2 and 3.\nMMTC was an important public-sector trading enterprise, particularly in minerals and metals.","revisionPoints":"MMTC → Minerals and Metals Trading Corporation.\nPSU questions often test:\nParent ministry\nMain function\nSector of operation\nHistorical status\nExport credit institutions are associated with facilitating and protecting exports."},
{"no":86,"text":"Match the books with their authors:\nBook\nAuthor\nA. Autobiography of an Unknown Indian\n1. Mulk Raj Anand\nB. India: A Wounded Civilization\n2. Nirad C. Chaudhuri\nC. Confessions of a Lover\n3. R. K. Narayan\nD. The English Teacher\n4. V. S. Naipaul\nCodes:","A":"1 3 2 4","B":"2 4 1 3","C":"1 4 2 3","D":"2 3 1 4","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Books & Authors","tags":["Indian Writing in English","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Autobiography of an Unknown Indian → Nirad C. Chaudhuri (2).\nIndia: A Wounded Civilization → V. S. Naipaul (4).\nConfessions of a Lover → Mulk Raj Anand (1).\nThe English Teacher → R. K. Narayan (3).\nTherefore, the sequence is 2–4–1–3.","revisionPoints":"Nirad C. Chaudhuri → Autobiography of an Unknown Indian.\nV. S. Naipaul → India: A Wounded Civilization.\nR. K. Narayan → The English Teacher.\nLiterature questions often use book–author matching."},
{"no":87,"text":"With reference to Indian Public Finance, consider the following statements:\nExternal liabilities reported in the Union Budget are based on historical exchange rates.\nContinued high borrowing has kept real interest rates high in the economy.\nThe upward trend in the ratio of Fiscal Deficit to GDP has an adverse effect on private investment.\nInterest payments are the single largest component of the non-plan revenue expenditure of the Union Government.\nWhich of these are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 4","C":"2, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Finance","tags":["Fiscal Deficit","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 2 — Correct: High government borrowing can contribute to upward pressure on interest rates.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Persistent fiscal deficits can crowd out private investment.\nStatement 4 — Correct: Interest payments formed a major component of Union Government non-plan revenue expenditure.\nThe source therefore selects 2, 3 and 4.","revisionPoints":"Fiscal deficit → excess of government expenditure over relevant receipts.\nHigh government borrowing can create a crowding-out effect.\nInterest payments → major government expenditure.\nFiscal deficit is commonly expressed as a percentage of GDP."},
{"no":88,"text":"With reference to Indian Polity, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Planning Commission is accountable to Parliament.","B":"President can make an ordinance only when either of the two Houses of Parliament is not in session.","C":"The minimum age prescribed for appointment as a Judge of the Supreme Court is 40 years.","D":"National Development Council is constituted of the Union Finance Minister and the Chief Ministers of all the States.","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Ordinance Making Power","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The President's ordinance-making power is provided under Article 123.\nAn ordinance can be promulgated when both Houses are not simultaneously in session.\nThe other statements are not correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Article 123 → President's ordinance-making power.\nOrdinance has the force of an Act of Parliament.\nIt must be laid before Parliament when it reassembles.\nOrdinance-making power is an executive legislative power."},
{"no":89,"text":"The correct sequence of the eastward-flowing rivers of Peninsular India from north to south is:","A":"Subarnarekha, Mahanadi, Godavari, Krishna, Pennar, Cauvery and Vaigai","B":"Subarnarekha, Mahanadi, Godavari, Cauvery, Vaigai, Pennar","C":"Mahanadi, Subarnarekha, Godavari, Krishna, Cauvery, Pennar and Vaigai","D":"Mahanadi, Subarnarekha, Krishna, Godavari, Cauvery, Vaigai and Pennar","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["Peninsular Rivers","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"These rivers can be arranged geographically from north to south.\nSubarnarekha lies farther north.\nMahanadi follows southward.\nGodavari and Krishna are major east-flowing rivers of central Peninsular India.\nPennar, Cauvery and Vaigai occur progressively farther south.","revisionPoints":"Major east-flowing Peninsular rivers:\nSubarnarekha\nMahanadi\nGodavari\nKrishna\nPennar\nCauvery\nVaigai\nMost major Peninsular rivers drain into the Bay of Bengal.\nRiver-order questions require attention to latitude and geographical position."},
{"no":90,"text":"The world's highest ground-based telescopic observatory is located in:","A":"Colombia","B":"India","C":"Nepal","D":"Switzerland","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["Hanle Observatory","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies India as the answer.\nHigh-altitude observatories benefit from atmospheric conditions that can improve astronomical observations.\nThe question is based on the status of observatories at the time the paper was published.","revisionPoints":"High-altitude locations are advantageous for astronomical observation.\nFactors include:\nThin atmosphere\nLow atmospheric moisture\nReduced atmospheric disturbance\nAstronomical observatories are often located in remote, high-altitude regions."},
{"no":91,"text":"Two ladies simultaneously leave cities A and B connected by a straight road and travel towards each other. The first lady travels 2 km/hr faster than the second lady and reaches B one hour before the second lady reaches A. The two cities are 24 km apart. How many kilometres does each lady travel in one hour?","A":"5 km, 3 km","B":"7 km, 5 km","C":"8 km, 6 km","D":"16 km, 14 km","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Let the slower woman's speed = x km/hr.\nFaster woman's speed = x + 2 km/hr.\nThe distance is 24 km.\nAccording to the given condition, the difference between their travel times is 1 hour:\n\\[ \\frac{24}{x}-\\frac{24}{x+2}=1 \\]\nSolving gives:\n\\[ x=6 \\]\nFaster speed = 8 km/hr.","revisionPoints":"Speed = Distance / Time.\nFor same distance, higher speed means less travel time.\nAge/speed questions can be solved by setting up equations from the difference in time.\nCorrect speeds → 8 km/hr and 6 km/hr."},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following names:\nArchbishop Desmond Tutu\nLech Walesa\nShimon Peres\nYasser Arafat\nWho among these won the Nobel Peace Prize?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1, 3 and 4","C":"2, 3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Nobel Prizes","tags":["Nobel Peace Prize","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Desmond Tutu → Nobel Peace Prize, 1984.\nLech Walesa → Nobel Peace Prize, 1983.\nShimon Peres → Nobel Peace Prize, 1994.\nYasser Arafat → Nobel Peace Prize, 1994.\nHence, all four are correct.","revisionPoints":"Desmond Tutu → anti-apartheid movement.\nLech Walesa → Polish Solidarity movement.\nShimon Peres and Yasser Arafat → 1994 Nobel Peace Prize jointly with Yitzhak Rabin.\nNobel Peace Prize → awarded for contributions to peace and conflict resolution."},
{"no":93,"text":"With which one of the following mountain tribes did the British first come into contact after the grant of Diwani in 1765?","A":"Garo","B":"Khasis","C":"Kukis","D":"Tipperahs","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["North East Tribes","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies the Khasis as the correct answer.\nThe question places the event in the context of the British acquisition of Diwani rights in 1765.\nThe Khasi hills formed an important frontier region in northeastern India.","revisionPoints":"Diwani rights → granted to the East India Company in 1765.\nDiwani → right to collect revenue in Bengal, Bihar and Orissa.\nKhasi → important hill community of Northeast India.\nColonial expansion gradually brought the British into contact with various hill communities."},
{"no":94,"text":"With reference to Indian transport systems, consider the following statements:\nIndian railway system is the largest in the world.\nNational Highways cater to 45% of the total road transport demand.\nAmong the States, Kerala has the highest density of surface roads.\nNational Highway No. 7 is the longest in the country.\nWhich of these are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"2 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Transport & Infrastructure","tags":["National Highways","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source accepts Statements 2 and 4.\nNational Highways, despite forming a relatively small part of the total road network, carry a large share of road traffic.\nAt the time of the question, NH-7 was regarded as India's longest National Highway.","revisionPoints":"National Highways → important inter-city road network.\nNH-7 historically connected Varanasi to Kanyakumari.\nRoad density varies significantly among Indian States.\nTransport questions often depend on the historical year of the data."},
{"no":95,"text":"Match List I with List II:\nName\nAchievement\nA. Chinna Pillai\n1. Winner of Neerja Bhanot Award\nB. Kaveri Thakur Puraskar\n2. Recipient of Stree Shakti\nC. Mira Nair\n3. Became the fastest swimmer in Asia at age 16\nD. Yasoda Ekambaram\n4. Recipient of the Golden Lion at Venice Film Festival\nCodes:","A":"3 2 1 4","B":"2 3 4 1","C":"3 2 4 1","D":"2 3 1 4","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Awards","tags":["Notable Women","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"According to the source's matching:\nChinna Pillai → Stree Shakti (2).\nKaveri Thakur Puraskar → fastest swimmer in Asia at age 16 (3).\nMira Nair → Golden Lion at Venice Film Festival (4).\nYasoda Ekambaram → Neerja Bhanot Award (1).\nTherefore, the sequence is 2–3–4–1.","revisionPoints":"Mira Nair → internationally recognised Indian filmmaker.\nGolden Lion → top award associated with the Venice Film Festival.\nStree Shakti → recognition associated with women's empowerment.\nAwards and personalities are often tested through matching questions."},
{"no":96,"text":"With reference to the government's welfare schemes, consider the following statements:\nUnder the Antyodaya Anna Yojana, food grains were available to the poorest of the poor families at ₹2/kg for wheat and ₹3/kg for rice.\nUnder the National Old Age Pension Scheme, old and destitute persons were provided ₹75 per month as Central pension, in addition to the amount provided by most State Governments.\nGovernment of India allocated 25 kg food grains per Below Poverty Line family per month at less than half the economic cost.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Antyodaya Anna Yojana","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies all three statements as correct.\nAntyodaya Anna Yojana targeted the poorest of the poor.\nThe National Old Age Pension Scheme provided central assistance to eligible elderly persons.\nFood-grain allocations for BPL families formed part of the public distribution/welfare framework.","revisionPoints":"Antyodaya Anna Yojana → targeted the poorest households.\nNational Old Age Pension Scheme → social security support for elderly persons.\nBPL → Below Poverty Line.\nFood security schemes are important examples of targeted welfare intervention."},
{"no":97,"text":"Consider the following countries:\nBrazil\nIndonesia\nJapan\nRussia\nWhat is the descending order of their population size?","A":"1, 2, 4, 3","B":"2, 3, 1, 4","C":"2, 1, 4, 3","D":"1, 2, 3, 4","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["World Population","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"According to the source:\nIndonesia → 2\nBrazil → 1\nRussia → 4\nJapan → 3\nTherefore, the descending order is:\nIndonesia → Brazil → Russia → Japan","revisionPoints":"Population rankings are time-sensitive.\nIndonesia → Southeast Asia.\nBrazil → South America.\nRussia → spans Europe and Asia.\nJapan → East Asia."},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following countries:\nAngola\nColombia\nCongo\nSudan\nWhich of these countries has/have been suffering from civil war?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Civil Wars","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The supplied source gives Sudan as the answer.\nThe question is based on the geopolitical situation prevailing when the 2002 paper was prepared.\nBecause civil conflicts can change over time, historical questions like this must be interpreted in their contemporary context.","revisionPoints":"Civil war → armed conflict occurring within a state.\nQuestions on conflicts should always be linked to the time period mentioned or implied.\nSudan has historically experienced prolonged internal conflicts.\nGeopolitical questions are highly time-sensitive."},
{"no":99,"text":"Who among the following was the first Bhakti saint to use Hindi for the propagation of his message?","A":"Dadu","B":"Kabir","C":"Ramananda","D":"Tulsidas","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Bhakti Movement","tags":["Bhakti Movement","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Ramananda as the answer.\nRamananda played an important role in the North Indian Bhakti tradition.\nHis teachings helped broaden the devotional movement beyond narrow social boundaries.\nThe use of a language accessible to ordinary people helped spread Bhakti ideas.","revisionPoints":"Ramananda → important figure in North Indian Bhakti tradition.\nKabir → prominent Nirguna Bhakti saint.\nTulsidas → associated with Ramcharitmanas.\nBhakti movement emphasised personal devotion."},
{"no":100,"text":"In a bisexual flower, if androecium and gynoecium mature at different times, the phenomenon is known as:","A":"Dichogamy","B":"Herkogamy","C":"Heterogamy","D":"Monogamy","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Botany","tags":["Flower Biology","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Dichogamy occurs when the male and female reproductive organs of a bisexual flower mature at different times.\nThis reduces the possibility of self-pollination.\nIt is an important mechanism promoting cross-pollination.","revisionPoints":"Dichogamy → different maturation times of anthers and stigma.\nTwo forms:\nProtandry → anthers mature first.\nProtogyny → stigma matures first.\nHerkogamy → spatial separation of anther and stigma.\nBoth dichogamy and herkogamy can promote cross-pollination."},
{"no":101,"text":"The average annual temperature of a meteorological station is 26°C, its average annual rainfall is 63 cm, and the annual range of temperature is 9°C. The station in question is:","A":"Allahabad","B":"Chennai","C":"Cherrapunji","D":"Kolkata","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Climate Data","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Allahabad as the correct answer.\nThe station is characterised by:\nAverage annual temperature → 26°C\nAnnual rainfall → 63 cm\nAnnual temperature range → 9°C\nThese climatic values are used to identify the location.","revisionPoints":"Climate identification questions use:\nTemperature\nRainfall\nAnnual temperature range\nPeninsular/coastal locations generally show lower annual temperature ranges.\nInterior locations generally experience greater seasonal variation."},
{"no":102,"text":"With reference to the period of the Extremist Nationalist Movement in India and its spirit of Swadeshi, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Laikat Hossain led the Muslim peasants of Barisal in their agitations.","B":"In 1989, the scheme of national education was formulated by Satish Chandra Mukherjee.","C":"The Bengal National College was founded in 1906 with Aurobindo as the Principal.","D":"Tagore preached the cult of Atmasakti, the main plank of which was social and economic regeneration of the villages.","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Swadeshi Movement","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Laikat Hossain as the correct answer.\nThe question belongs to the Swadeshi and Extremist phase of Indian nationalism.\nThe period was associated with:\nBoycott\nSwadeshi\nNational education\nPolitical mobilisation","revisionPoints":"Swadeshi Movement → strongly associated with the Partition of Bengal, 1905.\nMajor methods:\nBoycott of foreign goods\nPromotion of indigenous goods\nNational education\nExtremist leaders emphasised self-reliance and mass mobilisation.\nSource note: Statement (b) contains an obvious OCR/date error (“1989”); the supplied source nevertheless gives (a) as the answer."},
{"no":103,"text":"In the Indian Constitution, the Right to Equality is granted by five Articles. They are:","A":"Article 16 to Article 20","B":"Article 15 to Article 19","C":"Article 14 to Article 18","D":"Article 13 to Article 17","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["Right to Equality","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Right to Equality is covered by Articles 14–18.\nThese provisions deal with:\nEquality before law\nProhibition of discrimination\nEquality of opportunity in public employment\nAbolition of untouchability\nAbolition of titles","revisionPoints":"Article 14 → Equality before law.\nArticle 15 → Prohibition of discrimination.\nArticle 16 → Equality of opportunity in public employment.\nArticle 17 → Abolition of untouchability.\nArticle 18 → Abolition of titles."},
{"no":104,"text":"Amit started a business by investing ₹30,000. Rahul joined the business after some time and invested ₹20,000. At the end of the year, profit was divided in the ratio 2 : 1. After how many months did Rahul join the business?","A":"2 months","B":"3 months","C":"4 months","D":"5 months","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Profit is divided according to capital × time.\nAmit's investment = ₹30,000 for 12 months.\nRahul's investment = ₹20,000 for x months.\nProfit ratio:\n\\[ \\frac{30000\\times12}{20000\\times x}=\\frac{2}{1} \\]\nSimplifying:\n\\[ \\frac{18}{x}=2 \\]\\[ x=9 \\]\nTherefore, Rahul joined 9 months before the end of the year, i.e. after 3 months from the beginning.","revisionPoints":"Partnership profit ∝ Investment × Time.\nIf investment periods differ, simply comparing capital is incorrect.\nAmit's capital-time product:\n30,000 × 12 = 3,60,000\nRahul's required capital-time product for a 1:2 share = 1,80,000, requiring 9 months.\nHence Rahul joined after 3 months."},
{"no":105,"text":"The time in a wall clock is 3:25. The acute angle between the hour hand and the minute hand is:","A":"60°","B":"52½°","C":"47½°","D":"42°","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Minute hand at 25 minutes:\n\\[ 25\\times6=150^\\circ \\]\nHour hand at 3:25:\n\\[ 3\\times30 + 25\\times0.5 =90+12.5 =102.5^\\circ \\]\nDifference:\n\\[ 150-102.5=47.5^\\circ \\]\nTherefore, the acute angle is 47½°.","revisionPoints":"Minute hand moves 6° per minute.\nHour hand moves 0.5° per minute.\nHour hand does not remain fixed at the hour mark.\nFormula:\nAngle = |30H − 5.5M|"},
{"no":106,"text":"Which one of the following amendments to the Indian Constitution empowers the President to send back any matter for reconsideration by the Council of Ministers?","A":"39th","B":"40th","C":"42nd","D":"44th","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendments","tags":["44th Amendment","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The 44th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1978 modified the President's position regarding the advice of the Council of Ministers.\nThe President may once require the Council of Ministers to reconsider its advice.\nAfter reconsideration, the President must act according to the advice given.","revisionPoints":"42nd Amendment → strengthened the binding nature of ministerial advice.\n44th Amendment → President can return advice once for reconsideration.\nAfter reconsideration → President is bound to act according to the advice.\nArticle 74 → relationship between President and Council of Ministers."},
{"no":107,"text":"Recently, several hundred South African soldiers were sent to Burundi:","A":"To side with the Tutsi tribe in the country's civil war","B":"To side with the Hutu tribe in the ongoing ethnic conflict","C":"As a part of a United Nations special force to control ethnic groups revolting against the government","D":"As South Africa's protection force to help mediate an agreement between warring groups in the civil war","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["Burundi","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies (d).\nSouth African forces were involved in the Burundi peace process.\nTheir role was connected with supporting peace negotiations and protecting political leaders, rather than taking sides in the ethnic conflict.","revisionPoints":"Burundi → Great Lakes region of Africa.\nConflict involved major Hutu–Tutsi tensions.\nSouth Africa played a role in regional peace efforts.\nPeacekeeping and mediation are distinct from taking sides in a civil conflict."},
{"no":108,"text":"Chapchar Kut is a festival celebrated in the state of:","A":"Arunachal Pradesh","B":"Assam","C":"Mizoram","D":"Sikkim","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Festivals & Traditions","tags":["Mizoram","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Chapchar Kut is a major traditional festival of Mizoram.\nIt is associated with the completion of the jungle-clearing phase of shifting cultivation.\nIt is an important cultural festival of the Mizo people.","revisionPoints":"Chapchar Kut → Mizoram.\nIt is associated with jhum/shifting cultivation traditions.\nNortheast India has numerous distinctive tribal and cultural festivals.\nState–festival matching is a common UPSC theme."},
{"no":109,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nNTPD is the largest power utility in India.\nONGC accounts for half of the LPG production in India.\nIndian Oil Corporation operates all the oil refineries in India.\nThe Indian Ordnance Factory is the largest departmentally run industrial undertaking in the country.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3","C":"2, 3 and 4","D":"1 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Sector Undertakings","tags":["NTPC","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Statements 1 and 4 as correct.\nThe source refers to the largest power utility as NTPD; this appears to be an OCR rendering of NTPC.\nStatement 4 refers to the Ordnance Factory organisation as the largest departmentally run industrial undertaking.","revisionPoints":"NTPC → major public-sector power generation company.\nONGC → major petroleum and natural gas enterprise.\nIndian Oil Corporation → major oil refining and marketing company, but not the operator of every refinery in India.\nOrdnance factories → historically associated with defence production."},
{"no":110,"text":"Match the oxidation number with the corresponding element:\nOxidation Number\nElement\nA. 2\n1. Mn in MnO₂\nB. 3\n2. S in H₂S₂O₇\nC. 4\n3. Ca in CaO₂\nD. 6\n4. Al in NaAlH₄\nCodes:","A":"3 4 1 2","B":"4 3 1 2","C":"3 4 2 1","D":"4 3 2 1","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Oxidation Numbers","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Ca in CaO₂ → +2 → A–3.\nAl in NaAlH₄ → +3 → B–4.\nMn in MnO₂ → +4 → C–1.\nS in H₂S₂O₇ → +6 → D–2.\nHence, 3–4–1–2.","revisionPoints":"Mn in MnO₂ → +4.\nCa in calcium peroxide → +2.\nAl in NaAlH₄ → +3.\nOxidation number is determined using the standard oxidation-state rules."},
{"no":111,"text":"With reference to the Indian freedom struggle, which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Hakim Ajmal Khan was one of the leaders to start a nationalist and militant Ahrar movement.","B":"When the Indian National Congress was formed, Sayyid Ahmad Khan opposed it.","C":"The All-India Muslim League, formed in 1906, vehemently opposed the partition of Bengal and separate electorates.","D":"Maulana Barkatullah and Maulana Obaidullah Sindhi were among those who formed a Provisional Government of India in Kabul.","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Provisional Government of India","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source marks (c) as the answer.\nThe question tests knowledge of political organisations during the freedom struggle.\nThe Muslim League was founded in 1906.\nThe supplied source associates the correct statement with its early political position.","revisionPoints":"All-India Muslim League → founded in 1906.\nFormation → associated with Dacca (Dhaka).\nEarly constitutional politics involved questions of:\nRepresentation\nSeparate electorates\nCommunal political interests\nFreedom-struggle questions often test the evolution of political organisations.\nSource note: This question's wording and answer key appear historically inconsistent in the supplied text. The answer above follows the provided source key, as requested."},
{"no":112,"text":"The Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) Forum was held in the year 2001 in:","A":"Bangkok","B":"Hong Kong","C":"Jakarta","D":"Shanghai","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Organizations","tags":["APEC","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The 2001 APEC Economic Leaders' Meeting was held in Shanghai, China.\nAPEC is a regional economic forum focused on economic cooperation and trade in the Asia-Pacific region.","revisionPoints":"APEC → Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation.\nAPEC focuses on:\nTrade\nInvestment\nEconomic cooperation\n2001 host → Shanghai, China.\nInternational organisation questions often test venue + year."},
{"no":113,"text":"Match the ethnic communities with their countries:\nEthnic Community\nCountry\nA. Apatani\n1. China\nB. Dayak\n2. India\nC. Dinka\n3. Indonesia\nD. Uighur\n4. Sudan\nCodes:","A":"2 3 4 1","B":"3 2 4 1","C":"2 3 1 4","D":"3 2 1 4","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Ethnic Communities","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Apatani → India (2).\nDayak → Indonesia (3).\nDinka → Sudan (4).\nUighur → China (1).\nTherefore, the sequence is 2–3–4–1.","revisionPoints":"Apatani → indigenous community of Arunachal Pradesh.\nDayak → indigenous peoples of Borneo, including Indonesia.\nDinka → major ethnic group of South Sudan/Sudan region.\nUighurs → Turkic ethnic community primarily associated with Xinjiang, China."},
{"no":114,"text":"The term of the Lok Sabha:","A":"Cannot be extended under any circumstances","B":"Can be extended by six months at a time","C":"Can be extended by one year at a time during the proclamation of Emergency","D":"Can be extended for two years at a time during the proclamation of Emergency","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["Term of Lok Sabha","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The normal term of the Lok Sabha is five years.\nDuring a proclamation of Emergency, Parliament may extend its term.\nSuch extension can be made one year at a time.\nThe extension cannot continue beyond six months after the Emergency has ceased.","revisionPoints":"Lok Sabha normal term → 5 years.\nEmergency → term can be extended one year at a time.\nExtension cannot continue indefinitely after Emergency ends.\nRelated provision → Article 83."},
{"no":115,"text":"In the year 2001, Germany approved a $32 million credit to India:","A":"To promote primary education in selected States","B":"For Tehri Dam project","C":"To assist in the development of nuclear energy","D":"For oceanographic research","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"India's Foreign Relations","tags":["India-Germany Relations","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies oceanographic research as the purpose of the German credit.\nInternational financial assistance may be directed towards specialised scientific and research activities.\nThe question is based on a 2001 contemporary-affairs event.","revisionPoints":"Oceanography → scientific study of oceans.\nIncludes:\nMarine geology\nMarine biology\nPhysical oceanography\nOcean chemistry\nContemporary-affairs questions should be interpreted according to the period of the paper."},
{"no":116,"text":"Consider the following:\nCurrency with the public\nDemand deposits with banks\nTime deposits with banks\nWhich of these are included in Broad Money (M3) in India?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["Broad Money","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Broad Money (M3) includes:\nCurrency with the public\nDemand deposits with the banking system\nTime deposits with the banking system\nTherefore, all three components are included.","revisionPoints":"M1 → narrow measure of money supply.\nM3 → commonly referred to as Broad Money.\nTime deposits are an important addition when moving from narrow to broad money.\nMoney supply measures are important tools for understanding monetary conditions."},
{"no":117,"text":"Who among the following won six gold medals in the 9th FINA World Swimming Championship held in Fukuoka, Japan, in 2001?","A":"Grant Hackett","B":"Ian Thorpe","C":"Michael Phelps","D":"Roman Sloudnov","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Swimming","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Ian Thorpe as the answer.\nThe 9th FINA World Championships were held in Fukuoka, Japan, in 2001.\nThorpe was one of the dominant swimmers at the championship.","revisionPoints":"Ian Thorpe → Australian swimmer.\nFINA → international governing body historically associated with aquatic sports.\nFukuoka → Japan.\nSports questions in old UPSC papers often test contemporary international achievements."},
{"no":118,"text":"Foot-and-mouth disease in animals is caused by:","A":"Bacterium","B":"Fungus","C":"Protozoan","D":"Virus","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Veterinary Science","tags":["Foot and Mouth Disease","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Foot-and-mouth disease is a viral disease affecting cloven-hoofed animals.\nIt is highly contagious.\nIt can affect livestock such as cattle, pigs, sheep and goats.","revisionPoints":"Foot-and-mouth disease → viral disease.\nAffects cloven-hoofed animals.\nHighly contagious livestock disease.\nDo not confuse it with diseases caused by bacteria, fungi or protozoa."},
{"no":119,"text":"In the year 2001, India offered a grant of five million dollars to Tajikistan to:","A":"Tackle the drought situation","B":"Set up a Software Technology Park","C":"Promote mineral exploration","D":"Procure defence equipment","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"India's Foreign Relations","tags":["India-Tajikistan Relations","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies drought relief as the purpose of the grant.\nThe assistance represented India's development and humanitarian support to Tajikistan.\nThe question is based on a contemporary international-relations event.","revisionPoints":"Tajikistan → Central Asia.\nIndia has historically maintained development and diplomatic relations with Central Asian countries.\nForeign assistance can be directed towards:\nHumanitarian relief\nDevelopment\nInfrastructure\nCapacity building"},
{"no":120,"text":"Consider the following countries:\nAlbania\nBosnia-Herzegovina\nCroatia\nMacedonia\nWhich of these countries has/have the Adriatic Sea as a boundary?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"4 only","D":"3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Adriatic Sea","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Albania → Adriatic Sea\nBosnia-Herzegovina → Adriatic Sea\nCroatia → Adriatic Sea\nMacedonia → landlocked\nTherefore, 1, 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Adriatic Sea → part of the Mediterranean Sea.\nLies between the Italian Peninsula and the Balkan Peninsula.\nCountries with Adriatic coastline include:\nSlovenia\nCroatia\nBosnia-Herzegovina\nMontenegro\nAlbania\nItaly\nMacedonia (North Macedonia) → landlocked."},
{"no":121,"text":"A hollow sphere of radius R, a hollow cube of side R, and a thin circular plate of radius R, all made of the same material, are heated to 20°C above room temperature. Which one will reach room temperature first?","A":"Circular plate","B":"Cube","C":"Sphere","D":"All will reach room temperature at the same time","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Heat Transfer","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Cooling depends on the relationship between surface area and volume.\nA body with greater effective surface area relative to its volume loses heat faster.\nAmong the given bodies, the source identifies the sphere as reaching room temperature first.","revisionPoints":"Heat loss depends on surface area, temperature difference and surrounding conditions.\nSurface area-to-volume ratio is important in cooling.\nSmaller characteristic dimensions generally favour faster cooling."},
{"no":122,"text":"Arrange the following financial institutions in the correct chronological order of their establishment:\nIFCI\nICICI\nIDBI\nNABARD","A":"1, 2, 3, 4","B":"2, 3, 4, 1","C":"3, 4, 1, 2","D":"4, 1, 2, 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Institutions","tags":["IFCI","ICICI","IDBI","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"IFCI was established first.\nICICI was established subsequently.\nIDBI was established after ICICI.\nNABARD was established last among the four.","revisionPoints":"IFCI — Industrial Finance Corporation of India.\nICICI — Industrial Credit and Investment Corporation of India.\nIDBI — Industrial Development Bank of India.\nNABARD — National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development.\nChronology-based questions require attention to establishment years."},
{"no":123,"text":"The Khudai Khidmatgars, also known as the Red Shirts, called for:","A":"Union of the Pashtun tribal areas of the North-West with Afghanistan","B":"Adoption of terrorist tactics against the British","C":"Adoption of communist revolutionary ideology","D":"Pathan regional nationalist unity and struggle against colonialism","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Khudai Khidmatgar","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Khudai Khidmatgar movement was associated with Abdul Ghaffar Khan.\nIt mobilised the Pathan/Pashtun population against British colonial rule.\nThe movement emphasised non-violent resistance.\nIt became closely associated with the Indian national movement.","revisionPoints":"Khudai Khidmatgar = Red Shirts.\nAssociated with Abdul Ghaffar Khan.\nStrong base in the North-West Frontier Province.\nFollowed a predominantly non-violent method of struggle.\nImportant for understanding regional participation in the freedom movement."},
{"no":124,"text":"Match List I (Commission) with List II (Matter of Enquiry):\nList I\nList II\nA. Wadhwa Commission\n1. The assistance of Rajiv Gandhi\nB. Liberhan Commission\n2. Killing of Graham Staines\nC. Sri Krishna Commission\n3. Demolition of a religious structure at Ayodhya\nD. Jain Commission\n4. Riots in Mumbai in 1993\nCodes:","A":"3 2 4 1","B":"2 3 4 1","C":"3 2 1 4","D":"2 3 1 4","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Commissions of Inquiry","tags":["Liberhan Commission","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Wadhwa Commission → Killing of Graham Staines.\nLiberhan Commission → Demolition of the religious structure at Ayodhya.\nSri Krishna Commission → Mumbai riots of 1993.\nJain Commission → Rajiv Gandhi assassination-related enquiry.","revisionPoints":"Wadhwa Commission — Graham Staines killing.\nLiberhan Commission — Ayodhya demolition.\nSri Krishna Commission — 1992–93 Mumbai riots.\nJain Commission — Rajiv Gandhi assassination.\nCommission–event matching is a recurring UPSC factual area."},
{"no":125,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nIn equatorial regions, the year is divided into four main seasons.\nIn Mediterranean regions, summer receives more rain.\nIn China-type climate, rainfall occurs throughout the year.\nTropical highlands exhibit vertical zonation of different climates.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["World Climate Types","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Equatorial regions generally do not have four distinct conventional seasons.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: Mediterranean climates have dry summers and wetter winters.\nStatement 3 — Correct: China-type climate receives rainfall throughout the year.\nStatement 4 — Correct: Tropical highlands show climatic variation with altitude.","revisionPoints":"Equatorial climate — high temperature and rainfall throughout the year.\nMediterranean climate — dry summer, wet winter.\nChina-type climate — rainfall distributed through the year.\nAltitude creates vertical climatic zonation.\nClimate classification questions often test rainfall seasonality."},
{"no":126,"text":"A World Summit with representation from all countries was scheduled to be held in Johannesburg in September 2002 on the subject of:","A":"AIDS control","B":"Global terrorism","C":"Human rights","D":"Sustainable development","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Summits & Conferences","tags":["World Summit on Sustainable Development","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The summit referred to is the World Summit on Sustainable Development.\nIt was associated with the global effort to promote sustainable development.\nEnvironmental protection, economic development and social well-being were interconnected themes.","revisionPoints":"Johannesburg hosted the 2002 World Summit on Sustainable Development.\nSustainable development links:\nEconomic development\nSocial development\nEnvironmental protection\nIt followed the broader global environmental process initiated at Rio."},
{"no":127,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"All echinoderms are viviparous","B":"Round worm has no circulatory system","C":"In bony fishes, swim bladder is usually present","D":"In cartilaginous fishes, fertilisation is internal","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Zoology","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source marks (a) as the answer.\nEchinoderms include starfish, sea urchins and related organisms.\nThe question tests distinguishing characteristics of major animal groups.","revisionPoints":"Echinodermata includes starfish and sea urchins.\nRoundworms belong to Nematoda.\nSwim bladder is characteristic of many bony fishes.\nCartilaginous fishes include sharks and rays.\nSource note: The supplied source's answer key marks (a). Some of the biological statements are contestable under standard zoological classification, so this answer is being presented according to the source rather than silently replacing its key."},
{"no":128,"text":"Assume that the Moon takes exactly 30 days to complete its cycle and rises in the East exactly at 6:48 p.m. on the first day. On the fourth day, at what time will it rise?","A":"8:24 p.m.","B":"9:12 p.m.","C":"10:00 p.m.","D":"11:48 p.m.","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The Moon rises approximately 48 minutes later each successive day.\nFirst day = 6:48 p.m.\nSecond day = 7:36 p.m.\nThird day = 8:24 p.m.\nFourth day = 9:12 p.m.","revisionPoints":"Average daily delay in Moonrise ≈ 48–50 minutes.\nThe Moon completes a synodic cycle in approximately 29.5 days.\nMoonrise time changes daily because of the Moon's orbital motion."},
{"no":129,"text":"Match the scientists with their achievements:\nScientists\nAchievement\nA. Arber and Smith\n1. Developed transgenic plants with Agrobacterium T-DNA\nB. Feldman\n2. Discovered endonucleases\nC. Mullis\n3. Discovered reverse transcriptase\nD. Temin and Baltimore\n4. Discovered polymerase chain reaction\nCodes:","A":"2 1 4 3","B":"1 2 4 3","C":"2 1 3 4","D":"1 2 3 4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["Genetics Discoveries","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Arber and Smith → Endonucleases.\nFeldman → Transgenic plants using Agrobacterium T-DNA.\nMullis → Polymerase Chain Reaction (PCR).\nTemin and Baltimore → Reverse transcriptase.","revisionPoints":"PCR — associated with Kary Mullis.\nReverse transcriptase — associated with Temin and Baltimore.\nEndonucleases are important molecular biology enzymes.\nAgrobacterium T-DNA is important in plant genetic engineering."},
{"no":130,"text":"The Berber-speaking community, frequently in the news, lives in:","A":"Afghanistan","B":"Algeria","C":"Argentina","D":"Australia","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["Berber Community","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Berber-speaking populations are indigenous to North Africa.\nAlgeria has a significant Berber-speaking population.\nBerber languages belong to the Afro-Asiatic language family.","revisionPoints":"Berbers are indigenous peoples of North Africa.\nMajor Berber/Amazigh populations occur in:\nAlgeria\nMorocco\nLibya\nTunisia\nAmazigh is a commonly used self-designation."},
{"no":131,"text":"In the case of election to the Lok Sabha, the amount of security deposit for general category candidates and SC/ST category candidates, respectively, is:","A":"₹5,000 and ₹2,500","B":"₹10,000 and ₹2,500","C":"₹10,000 and ₹5,000","D":"₹15,000 and ₹7,500","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["Election Security Deposit","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The question uses the election rules applicable at the time of the 2002 examination.\nThe source specifies:\nGeneral category → ₹10,000\nSC/ST category → ₹5,000","revisionPoints":"Election security deposits are governed by election law.\nThe amount differs between general and SC/ST candidates.\nAlways consider the time period in historical polity questions because election rules can change."},
{"no":132,"text":"With reference to the power sector in India, consider the following statements:\nRural electrification has been treated as a Basic Minimum Service under the Prime Minister's Gramodaya Yojana.\n100 per cent Foreign Direct Investment in power is allowed without upper limit.\nThe Union Ministry of Power has signed Memoranda of Understanding with 14 states.\nWhich of these statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy Policy","tags":["Power Sector Reforms","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The source recognises 100% FDI in the power sector without an upper limit.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The source states that the Ministry of Power had signed MoUs with 14 states.","revisionPoints":"Power sector reforms include:\nGeneration\nTransmission\nDistribution\nRural electrification\nFDI policy in infrastructure has evolved over time.\nPolicy questions should be interpreted according to the period specified by the examination."},
{"no":133,"text":"A country is said to be in a debt trap if:","A":"It has to abide by the conditionalities imposed by the IMF","B":"It has to borrow to make interest payments on outstanding loans","C":"It has been refused loans or aid by creditors abroad","D":"The World Bank charges a very high rate of interest on outstanding as well as new loans","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["Debt Trap","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"A debt trap occurs when a country cannot service its existing debt from its available resources.\nIt therefore needs new borrowing to meet interest or debt-servicing obligations.\nContinued borrowing can increase the debt burden further.","revisionPoints":"Debt trap → borrowing required to service existing debt.\nDebt servicing includes payment of principal and/or interest.\nHigh external debt can create pressure on:\nFiscal resources\nForeign exchange\nBalance of payments\nDebt trap is different from merely having a high level of debt."},
{"no":134,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct?","A":"Prions are the smallest free-living cells","B":"The cell wall of Mycoplasmas is made up of amino sugars","C":"Viroids consist of a single-stranded RNA molecule","D":"Rickettsia lack cell wall","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Microorganisms","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Viroids are extremely small infectious agents consisting essentially of RNA.\nThey are smaller and structurally simpler than viruses.\nThey are especially associated with plant diseases.\nThe source identifies statement (c) as correct.","revisionPoints":"Viroids → small infectious RNA agents.\nPrions → infectious proteinaceous agents.\nMycoplasma → bacteria characterised by absence of a typical cell wall.\nRickettsia → small bacterial organisms associated with intracellular parasitism."},
{"no":135,"text":"Match the following countries with their Presidents:\nCountry\nPresident\nA. Congo\n1. Levy Mwanawasa\nB. Nigeria\n2. Joseph Kabila\nC. Uganda\n3. Olusegun Obasanjo\nD. Zambia\n4. Yoweri Museveni\nCodes:","A":"2 3 4 1","B":"3 2 1 4","C":"2 3 1 4","D":"3 2 4 1","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["African Presidents","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Congo → Joseph Kabila.\nNigeria → Olusegun Obasanjo.\nUganda → Yoweri Museveni.\nZambia → Levy Mwanawasa.","revisionPoints":"Joseph Kabila → Democratic Republic of the Congo.\nOlusegun Obasanjo → Nigeria.\nYoweri Museveni → Uganda.\nLevy Mwanawasa → Zambia.\nInternational current affairs questions often test country–leader matching."},
{"no":136,"text":"Consider the following ecosystems:\nTaiga\nTropical evergreen\nTropical deciduous\nTundra\nThe correct sequence in decreasing order of albedo values is:","A":"1, 4, 3, 2","B":"4, 1, 2, 3","C":"4, 1, 3, 2","D":"1, 4, 2, 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Ecosystems","tags":["Albedo","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Albedo refers to the fraction of incoming solar radiation reflected by a surface.\nSurfaces with greater reflectivity have higher albedo.\nThe source gives the decreasing sequence as:Tundra → Taiga → Tropical deciduous → Tropical evergreen.","revisionPoints":"High albedo → more solar radiation reflected.\nLow albedo → more solar energy absorbed.\nSnow and ice generally have high albedo.\nDense, dark vegetation generally has comparatively lower albedo."},
{"no":137,"text":"Consider the following statements. Full convertibility of the rupee may mean:\nIts free float with other international currencies.\nIts direct exchange with any other international currency at any prescribed place inside and outside the country.\nIt acts just like any other international currency.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["Rupee Convertibility","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Full convertibility implies greater freedom to exchange the domestic currency into foreign currencies.\nIt implies fewer restrictions on currency conversion.\nThe source treats all three statements as describing the implications of full convertibility.","revisionPoints":"Convertibility = ability to exchange domestic currency into foreign currency.\nCurrent account convertibility concerns transactions such as:\nTrade\nServices\nTransfers\nCapital account convertibility concerns movement of capital across borders.\nFull convertibility is a broader concept than current-account convertibility."},
{"no":138,"text":"The sea coast of which one of the following states became famous as a nesting place for the giant Olive Ridley turtles from South America?","A":"Goa","B":"Gujarat","C":"Orissa","D":"Tamil Nadu","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Wildlife","tags":["Olive Ridley Turtles","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies Orissa (now Odisha) as the answer.\nThe Odisha coast is famous for large-scale nesting of Olive Ridley sea turtles.\nMass nesting is known as arribada.","revisionPoints":"Olive Ridley turtle → Lepidochelys olivacea.\nMajor Indian nesting site → Odisha coast.\nGahirmatha is an important nesting area.\nArribada = mass nesting by sea turtles.\nMarine biodiversity and protected areas are important UPSC Environment topics."},
{"no":139,"text":"With reference to Indian satellites and their launchers, consider the following statements:\nAll the INSAT series of satellites were launched abroad.\nPSLVs were used to launch IRS series of satellites.\nIndia used indigenously built cryogenic engines for the first time for powering the third stage of GSLV.\nGSAT, launched in the year 2001, had payloads to demonstrate digital broadcasts and internet services.\nWhich of these statements are correct?","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"1 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["ISRO","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source.\nStatement 2 — Correct: PSLV was used for launching IRS-series satellites.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The source does not treat India as having used an indigenously built cryogenic engine for the GSLV third stage at that point.\nStatement 4 — Correct according to the source: GSAT was associated with experimental communication capabilities.","revisionPoints":"PSLV → Polar Satellite Launch Vehicle.\nGSLV → Geosynchronous Satellite Launch Vehicle.\nIRS → Indian Remote Sensing satellite series.\nINSAT → Indian National Satellite system.\nGSAT → communication satellite series.\nCryogenic technology is associated with high-efficiency upper-stage propulsion."},
{"no":140,"text":"The 93rd Constitutional Amendment Bill dealt with:","A":"Continuation of reservation for backward classes in government employment","B":"Free and compulsory education for all children between the age of 6 and 14 years","C":"Reservation of 30% posts for women in government recruitments","D":"Allocation of a greater number of parliamentary seats for recently created States","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendments","tags":["Right to Education","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"The source identifies the 93rd Constitutional Amendment Bill with free and compulsory education.\nThe proposal concerned children in the 6–14 years age group.\nIt was part of the constitutional development that ultimately led to the Right to Education framework.","revisionPoints":"Education was subsequently recognised as a Fundamental Right.\nArticle 21A deals with free and compulsory education for children aged 6–14 years.\nThe Right to Education framework is associated with the constitutional amendment process.\nDistinguish:\nFundamental Rights\nDirective Principles\nFundamental Duties\nConstitutional amendments\nSource note: The question and answer are reproduced according to the supplied 2002 source and its historical framing.\nUPSC-CSE Prelims General Studies 2002"},
{"no":141,"text":"Assertion (A): Drinking whisky increases the frequency of urination.Reason (R): Alcohol intake speeds up the secretion of vasopressin in the body.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Vasopressin","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Alcohol consumption can increase urine output.\nReason — Incorrect: Alcohol suppresses the action/secretion of vasopressin (ADH) rather than speeding it up.\nReduced ADH activity decreases water reabsorption by the kidneys.\nThis results in increased urine production.","revisionPoints":"Vasopressin = ADH (Antidiuretic Hormone).\nADH promotes water reabsorption in the kidneys.\nAlcohol reduces ADH activity.\nReduced ADH → increased urine output."},
{"no":142,"text":"Assertion (A): Muhammad bin Tughlaq left Delhi and, for two years, lived in a camp called Swargadwari.Reason (R): At that time, Delhi was ravaged by a form of plague and many people died.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["Muhammad bin Tughlaq","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct according to the source: Muhammad bin Tughlaq left Delhi and stayed at Swargadwari.\nReason — Incorrect according to the source: The reason given in the question is not accepted as the explanation.\nTherefore, the correct combination is A true, R false.","revisionPoints":"Muhammad bin Tughlaq belonged to the Tughlaq dynasty.\nHe is known for several administrative experiments.\nMajor events associated with his reign include:\nTransfer of capital\nToken currency\nTaxation measures\nMilitary expeditions\nAssertion–Reason questions require evaluating A and R independently."},
{"no":143,"text":"Assertion (A): With the increase of temperature, the viscosity of glycerine increases.Reason (R): Rise of temperature increases kinetic energy of molecules.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Viscosity","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — False: The viscosity of glycerine, like other liquids, generally decreases when temperature increases.\nReason — True: Increasing temperature increases the average kinetic energy of molecules.\nIncreased molecular motion reduces intermolecular resistance to flow in liquids.","revisionPoints":"Liquid viscosity decreases with increase in temperature.\nGas viscosity generally increases with temperature.\nHigher temperature → greater molecular kinetic energy.\nViscosity represents resistance to fluid flow."},
{"no":144,"text":"Assertion (A): The effect of labour participation in the Indian nationalist upsurge of the early 1930s was weak.Reason (R): The labour leaders considered the ideology of the Indian National Congress as bourgeois and reactionary.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["Labour Movement","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct according to the source: Labour participation in the nationalist upsurge of the early 1930s was relatively weak.\nReason — Correct: Sections of labour leadership viewed Congress ideology as bourgeois and reactionary.\nThis ideological difference weakened the degree of labour participation in the nationalist movement.","revisionPoints":"Indian labour movements developed alongside industrialisation.\nLabour politics was influenced by:\nNationalism\nSocialism\nCommunism\nTrade unionism\nThe relationship between the Congress and labour organisations was not always uniform.\nThe early 1930s were important for the interaction between nationalism and labour politics."},
{"no":145,"text":"Assertion (A): Synthetic detergents can lather well in hard water.Reason (R): Synthetic detergents form soluble calcium and magnesium salts with hard water.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["Detergents","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Synthetic detergents work effectively in hard water.\nReason — Correct according to the source: Their calcium and magnesium salts remain sufficiently soluble and do not form the insoluble scum characteristic of ordinary soaps.\nTherefore, detergents can produce lather even in hard water.","revisionPoints":"Hard water contains Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions.\nOrdinary soaps can form insoluble scum with these ions.\nSynthetic detergents generally remain effective in hard water.\nDetergents are widely used for washing because of their better performance in hard water."},
{"no":146,"text":"Assertion (A): 60°–65° latitudes in both hemispheres have a low-pressure belt instead of high pressure.Reason (R): The low-pressure areas are permanent over oceans rather than on land.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Sub-polar Low Pressure Belt","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Around 60°–65° latitude, there is a broad sub-polar low-pressure belt.\nReason — Incorrect: The existence of this pressure belt is not because low-pressure areas are permanently located over oceans.\nThe pressure belts are primarily related to global atmospheric circulation.","revisionPoints":"Equatorial region → Equatorial low pressure.\nAround 30° → Subtropical high pressure.\nAround 60° → Sub-polar low pressure.\nAround 90° → Polar high pressure.\nThese pressure belts are associated with global circulation cells."},
{"no":147,"text":"Assertion (A): Artificial satellites are always launched from the Earth in the eastward direction.Reason (R): The Earth rotates from west to east and so the satellite attains escape velocity.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Satellite Launch","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct according to the source: Satellites are generally launched eastward to take advantage of Earth's rotational velocity.\nReason — Incorrect: Earth's rotation provides an initial velocity advantage; it does not by itself give the satellite escape velocity.\nLaunching eastward reduces the energy required for orbital insertion.","revisionPoints":"Earth rotates from west to east.\nEastward launch receives assistance from Earth's rotational velocity.\nLaunch sites near the equator can obtain greater rotational velocity benefit.\nOrbital velocity and escape velocity are different concepts."},
{"no":148,"text":"Assertion (A): Human diet should compulsorily contain glycerine, serine and tyrosine.Reason (R): Essential amino acids cannot be synthesised in the human body.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Amino Acids","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — False: Glycerine is not an amino acid, while serine and tyrosine are generally considered non-essential amino acids because the body can synthesise them.\nReason — True: Essential amino acids cannot be synthesised adequately by the human body and therefore must be obtained through diet.\nHence, A is false and R is true.","revisionPoints":"Essential amino acids must be obtained from the diet.\nNon-essential amino acids can generally be synthesised by the body.\nSerine and tyrosine are amino acids.\nGlycerine (glycerol) is associated with lipids/fats, not amino acids."},
{"no":149,"text":"Assertion (A): The surface winds spiral inwards upon the centre of the cyclone.Reason (R): Air descends in the centre of the cyclone.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["Cyclones","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Surface winds in a cyclone generally spiral inward toward the centre.\nReason — Correct according to the source: The question treats descending air at the centre as a true statement.\nHowever, the descent of air at the centre does not explain the inward spiral of surface winds.\nThe inward spiral is associated with pressure-gradient force and Earth's rotation/Coriolis effect.","revisionPoints":"Cyclone → low-pressure system.\nSurface winds generally move toward the low-pressure centre.\nCoriolis force causes the winds to spiral.\nIn the Northern Hemisphere, cyclonic circulation is generally counter-clockwise.\nIn the Southern Hemisphere, it is generally clockwise."},
{"no":150,"text":"Assertion (A): An iron ball floats on mercury but gets immersed in water.Reason (R): The specific gravity of iron is more than that of mercury.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["Specific Gravity","UPSC","2002"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Iron is denser than water, so an iron ball sinks in water.\nIron is less dense than mercury, so it can float on mercury.\nReason — False: The specific gravity of iron is less than that of mercury.\nFloating depends on the relative densities of the object and the liquid.","revisionPoints":"Density of iron > density of water → iron sinks in water.\nDensity of iron < density of mercury → iron can float on mercury.\nSpecific gravity compares the density of a substance with a reference density.\nAn object floats when its average density is less than the density of the liquid."},
];
